/* EE311 · question banks, Modules 1-3 */
CONTENT.QBANK = CONTENT.QBANK.concat([

/* ===================== MODULE 1 — SIGNAL FOUNDATIONS (pp. 2–10) ===================== */

{ id:'Q1-01', module:'M1', kind:'concept', src:'pp. 2–3',
  stem:'The course uses the normalised ($R=1\\,\\Omega$) definitions $E_\\infty=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^{2}\\,dt$ and $P_\\infty=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^{2}\\,dt$. Which statement characterises an energy signal correctly?',
  opts:['Its total energy $E_\\infty$ is finite and its average power $P_\\infty$ is a finite non-zero constant.',
        'Its total energy $E_\\infty$ is finite, and consequently its average power $P_\\infty$ is zero.',
        'Its average power $P_\\infty$ is finite while its total energy grows without bound.',
        'It is bounded and of finite duration; a signal that lasts forever can never have finite energy.'],
  a:1,
  why:'If $E_\\infty<\\infty$, then $P_\\infty=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}E_\\infty/(2T)=0$: a fixed finite energy spread over an averaging window of growing length $2T$ gives zero average power.',
  wrong:{0:'Finite energy forces $P_\\infty=0$. A finite non-zero average power sustained over an infinite interval would already require infinite energy, so the two conditions cannot hold simultaneously.',
         2:'That is the definition of a power signal ($P_\\infty<\\infty$ with $E_\\infty\\to\\infty$) — the complementary class, not the energy class.',
         3:'Finite duration is sufficient but not necessary. The signal $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ lasts forever, yet $E_\\infty=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-2t}dt=1/2<\\infty$.'},
  hints:['Express $P_\\infty$ in terms of $E_\\infty$ for the case in which the energy integral converges.',
         'What is the limit of $E_\\infty/(2T)$ as $T\\to\\infty$ when $E_\\infty$ is a fixed finite number?'],
  sol:'Governing definitions :$$E_\\infty=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^{2}\\,dt,\\qquad P_\\infty=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^{2}\\,dt.$$Method: bound the power integral by the energy integral. For every $T$, $\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^{2}dt\\le E_\\infty$, hence$$0\\le\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^{2}\\,dt\\le\\frac{E_\\infty}{2T}.$$If $E_\\infty<\\infty$ the right-hand side tends to $0$, so by the squeeze argument $P_\\infty=0$ exactly.<br>Result: an energy signal satisfies both $E_\\infty<\\infty$ (joules, normalised) and $P_\\infty=0$ (watts) — this is the classification stated on p. 3.<br>Interpretation: energy signals are transient. All of their action is concentrated in an effectively finite stretch of time, so the long-run average power vanishes.<br>Verification with the definition example : $x(t)=1$ on $0\\le t\\le1$ and $0$ otherwise gives $E_\\infty=1$ J and $P_\\infty=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}1/(2T)=0$ — consistent.',
  err:'Quoting a non-zero average power for a finite-energy signal, usually by averaging over the support of the signal instead of over the window $[-T,T]$ with $T\\to\\infty$.',
  teach:'Insist on both halves of the classification; a student who writes only $E_\\infty<\\infty$ has not yet distinguished the energy class from the power class.' },

{ id:'Q1-02', module:'M1', kind:'concept', src:'p. 10',
  stem:'A discrete-time complex exponential $x[n]=Ce^{j\\omega_0 n}$ repeats only if $N=(2\\pi/\\omega_0)k$ is a positive integer for some integer $k$. Which of the following discrete-time signals is periodic?',
  opts:['$x[n]=\\cos(3n)$',
        '$x[n]=e^{j2n}$',
        '$x[n]=\\cos\\!\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{8}n\\right)$',
        '$x[n]=e^{jn/2}$'],
  a:2,
  why:'Here $\\omega_0/2\\pi=(3\\pi/8)/(2\\pi)=3/16$ is rational, so $N=(2\\pi/\\omega_0)k=(16/3)k$ is an integer for $k=3$, giving $N_0=16$ samples.',
  wrong:{0:'$\\omega_0=3$ rad/sample gives $\\omega_0/2\\pi=3/(2\\pi)$, an irrational number, so no positive integer $N$ satisfies $3N=2\\pi k$. Its continuous-time counterpart $\\cos(3t)$ is periodic with $T_0=2\\pi/3$, but the sampled sequence never repeats exactly.',
         1:'$\\omega_0=2$ gives $\\omega_0/2\\pi=1/\\pi$, again irrational; the sequence is aperiodic despite being built from a complex exponential.',
         3:'$\\omega_0=1/2$ gives $\\omega_0/2\\pi=1/(4\\pi)$, irrational. The samples come arbitrarily close to repeating but never repeat exactly, which is not periodicity.'},
  hints:['Periodicity in discrete time requires an integer $N$; the equation to satisfy is $\\omega_0N=2\\pi k$ with $N,k$ integers.',
         'Form the ratio $\\omega_0/2\\pi$ for each option and ask whether it is a ratio of two integers.'],
  sol:'Governing condition : $x[n]=x[n+N]$ requires $e^{j\\omega_0N}=1$, i.e. $\\omega_0N=2\\pi k$, so$$N=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega_0}k,\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb{Z},$$and $N$ must be a positive integer. Equivalently $\\dfrac{\\omega_0}{2\\pi}=\\dfrac{k}{N}\\in\\mathbb{Q}$.<br>Screening the options: $\\omega_0/2\\pi$ equals $3/(2\\pi)$, $1/\\pi$, $3/16$ and $1/(4\\pi)$ respectively. Only $3/16$ is rational.<br>For that option, $N=(16/3)k$ is an integer first at $k=3$, so $N_0=16$ samples and the fundamental frequency is $\\omega_0=2\\pi/N_0=\\pi/8$ rad/sample — consistent with the given $3\\pi/8$ being the third harmonic of $\\pi/8$.<br>Check: $\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{8}(n+16)\\right)=\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{8}n+6\\pi\\right)=\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{8}n\\right)$, and no smaller positive integer works because $16k/3$ is an integer only for $k$ a multiple of 3.',
  err:'Transferring the continuous-time rule $T_0=2\\pi/\\omega_0$ to discrete time and reporting a non-integer "period" such as $2\\pi/3$ for $\\cos(3n)$.',
  teach:'Require the rationality test $\\omega_0/2\\pi\\in\\mathbb{Q}$ to be written down explicitly; an answer that merely computes $2\\pi/\\omega_0$ has skipped the decisive step.' },

{ id:'Q1-03', module:'M1', kind:'concept', src:'pp. 6–7',
  stem:'The unit impulse obeys two distinct properties in continuous time. Which statement separates them correctly?',
  opts:['Both expressions evaluate to the number $x(t_0)$; sampling and sifting are two names for one operation.',
        'The sampling property returns the number $x(t_0)$, while the sifting property returns the scaled impulse $x(t_0)\\delta(t-t_0)$.',
        'The sampling property returns a signal, $x(t)\\delta(t-t_0)=x(t_0)\\delta(t-t_0)$, whereas the sifting property returns a number, $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)\\delta(t-t_0)\\,dt=x(t_0)$.',
        'The sampling property gives $x(t)\\delta(t-t_0)=x(t_0)$ for every $t$, so the product is an ordinary constant function of $t$.'],
  a:2,
  why:'Sampling is a multiplication, so its result is again a signal — an impulse at $t_0$ carrying the weight $x(t_0)$. Sifting integrates that product over all $t$ and therefore returns the scalar $x(t_0)$.',
  wrong:{0:'They are not the same operation: one output is a distribution supported at $t_0$, the other is a real (or complex) number. Only the integral collapses the impulse to a number.',
         1:'The roles are interchanged. Multiplication cannot produce a number, and integration over $t$ cannot leave a $t$-dependent impulse behind.',
         3:'Off $t=t_0$ the product is zero, so it certainly is not the constant $x(t_0)$. The correct product is the impulse $x(t_0)\\delta(t-t_0)$, whose weight (area) is $x(t_0)$.'},
  hints:['Ask what kind of object each side is: a function of $t$, or a single number?',
         'Multiplication by $\\delta(t-t_0)$ keeps a $t$-dependence; integration over $t$ removes it.'],
  sol:'Governing statements (pp. 6–7):$$\\text{sampling:}\\quad x(t)\\delta(t-t_0)=x(t_0)\\delta(t-t_0),\\qquad \\text{sifting:}\\quad \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)\\delta(t-t_0)\\,dt=x(t_0).$$Reasoning: $\\delta(t-t_0)$ is zero for $t\\neq t_0$, so the product can only be non-zero at $t=t_0$; there the factor $x(t)$ may be replaced by the constant $x(t_0)$, which is then pulled out of the impulse. The output is still a signal (an impulse of weight $x(t_0)$ located at $t_0$).<br>Integrating that same product uses $\\int\\delta(t-t_0)dt=1$, so $\\int x(t)\\delta(t-t_0)dt=x(t_0)\\int\\delta(t-t_0)dt=x(t_0)$ — a number, with the units of $x$.<br>Units check: if $x$ is in volts, then $\\delta$ has units of $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, the sampling product is in $\\mathrm{V\\,s^{-1}}$ (a signal), and the sifting integral is in volts (a number). The two results cannot be interchanged.<br>Discrete-time analogue : $x[n]\\delta[n-n_0]=x[n_0]\\delta[n-n_0]$ is a sequence, while $\\sum_{n}x[n]\\delta[n-n_0]=x[n_0]$ is a number — the same distinction without any distributional subtlety.',
  err:'Writing $x(t)\\delta(t-t_0)=x(t_0)$, which silently discards the impulse and converts a signal into a number.',
  teach:'Check that the student\'s answer keeps $\\delta(t-t_0)$ on the right-hand side of the sampling identity; losing it is the single most common notational slip in this chapter.' },

{ id:'Q1-04', module:'M1', kind:'calc', src:'pp. 2–3',
  stem:'Let $x(t)=3e^{-2t}u(t)$ volts, with the normalised ($R=1\\,\\Omega$) convention. Compute the total energy $E_\\infty$ and classify the signal.',
  opts:['$E_\\infty=2.25$ J with $P_\\infty=0$: an energy signal.',
        '$E_\\infty=4.5$ J with $P_\\infty=0$: an energy signal.',
        '$E_\\infty=1.5$ J with $P_\\infty=0$: an energy signal.',
        '$E_\\infty=2.25$ J with $P_\\infty=2.25$ W: a power signal.'],
  a:0,
  why:'$E_\\infty=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\bigl(3e^{-2t}\\bigr)^{2}dt=9\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-4t}dt=9/4=2.25$ J, which is finite, so $P_\\infty=0$ and the signal belongs to the energy class.',
  wrong:{1:'$4.5$ J results from squaring the amplitude but not the exponent: $\\int_{0}^{\\infty}9e^{-2t}dt=9/2$. Squaring $3e^{-2t}$ gives $9e^{-4t}$, so the decay rate doubles.',
         2:'$1.5$ J is $\\int_{0}^{\\infty}3e^{-2t}dt$ — the integral of the signal itself rather than of its squared magnitude.',
         3:'A finite energy forces $P_\\infty=0$; the value $2.25$ is the energy in joules, not a power. Averaging $2.25$ J over an infinite window gives zero watts.'},
  hints:['The step $u(t)$ only sets the lower limit of integration to $t=0$.',
         'Square the whole signal first: $(3e^{-2t})^{2}=9e^{-4t}$, not $9e^{-2t}$.'],
  sol:'Definition : $E_\\infty=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^{2}dt$ with $R=1\\,\\Omega$.<br>Step 1 — restrict the domain. Because $u(t)=0$ for $t<0$, the integral runs from $0$ to $\\infty$.<br>Step 2 — square the signal: $|x(t)|^{2}=\\bigl(3e^{-2t}\\bigr)^{2}=9e^{-4t}$.<br>Step 3 — integrate:$$E_\\infty=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}9e^{-4t}\\,dt=9\\left[\\frac{e^{-4t}}{-4}\\right]_{0}^{\\infty}=\\frac{9}{4}=2.25\\ \\text{J (normalised)}.$$Step 4 — power:$$P_\\infty=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{0}^{T}9e^{-4t}dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{9(1-e^{-4T})}{8T}=0\\ \\text{W}.$$Classification: $E_\\infty<\\infty$ and $P_\\infty=0$, so $x(t)$ is an energy signal.<br>Sanity check: the signal never exceeds 3 V and has decayed to about $2\\,\\%$ of its peak by $t=2$ s, so an energy of a few joule-equivalents is the right order; the exact value $9/4$ also follows from the standard result $\\int_{0}^{\\infty}A^{2}e^{-2at}dt=A^{2}/(2a)$ with $A=3$, $a=2$.',
  err:'Squaring only the amplitude and writing $\\int 9e^{-2t}dt=4.5$ J; the exponent must be doubled as well.',
  teach:'Look for the doubled decay rate in the integrand — it is the fastest way to see whether the student squared the signal or only its amplitude.' },

{ id:'Q1-05', module:'M1', kind:'calc', src:'pp. 2–3, 8',
  stem:'The signal $x(t)=5\\cos(4t-\\pi/3)$ volts is defined for all $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Determine its average power and classify it.',
  opts:['$P_\\infty=12.5$ W with $E_\\infty\\to\\infty$: a power signal.',
        '$P_\\infty=25$ W with $E_\\infty\\to\\infty$: a power signal.',
        '$P_\\infty=5$ W with $E_\\infty\\to\\infty$: a power signal.',
        '$E_\\infty=12.5$ J with $P_\\infty=0$: an energy signal.'],
  a:0,
  why:'Using $\\cos^{2}\\theta=\\tfrac12(1+\\cos2\\theta)$, the mean of $25\\cos^{2}(4t-\\pi/3)$ is $25/2=12.5$ W; the energy integral diverges because the signal never decays.',
  wrong:{1:'$25$ W is the peak instantaneous power $A^{2}$, not the average. The time average of $\\cos^{2}$ is $1/2$, so the mean power is $A^{2}/2$.',
         2:'$5$ W confuses the amplitude in volts with a power in watts; power is quadratic in the signal.',
         3:'A signal that oscillates forever has $E_\\infty\\to\\infty$. The number $12.5$ is a power in watts, obtained after dividing by the window length $2T$.'},
  hints:['Write $\\cos^{2}\\theta=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\cos2\\theta$ before integrating.',
         'Over a long window the $\\cos(8t-2\\pi/3)$ term contributes a bounded amount, which vanishes after division by $2T$.'],
  sol:'Definition : $P_\\infty=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^{2}dt$.<br>Step 1 — square and linearise:$$|x(t)|^{2}=25\\cos^{2}(4t-\\tfrac{\\pi}{3})=\\frac{25}{2}+\\frac{25}{2}\\cos\\!\\left(8t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right).$$Step 2 — integrate over $[-T,T]$: the constant contributes $25T$, while the oscillating term contributes $\\frac{25}{16}\\bigl[\\sin(8t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})\\bigr]_{-T}^{T}$, which is bounded by $25/8$ for every $T$.<br>Step 3 — divide and take the limit:$$P_\\infty=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{25T+\\text{(bounded)}}{2T}=\\frac{25}{2}=12.5\\ \\text{W}.$$Step 4 — energy: $\\int_{-T}^{T}|x|^{2}dt=25T+\\text{bounded}\\to\\infty$, so $E_\\infty\\to\\infty$.<br>Classification: $P_\\infty<\\infty$ and $E_\\infty\\to\\infty$, therefore a power signal.<br>Alternative verification: for any sinusoid the average power over one period $T_0=2\\pi/4=\\pi/2$ s already equals the infinite-interval average, and $\\frac{1}{T_0}\\int_{0}^{T_0}25\\cos^{2}(4t-\\pi/3)dt=25/2$ — the phase $-\\pi/3$ is irrelevant, as it only slides the waveform in time.',
  err:'Reporting $A^{2}=25$ W by forgetting the factor $1/2$ from the time average of $\\cos^{2}$.',
  teach:'Accept either the $\\cos^{2}$ identity or the one-period average, but require an explicit statement that the oscillatory term is bounded and therefore dies under the $1/(2T)$ scaling.' },

{ id:'Q1-06', module:'M1', kind:'calc', src:'pp. 5, 10',
  stem:'Find the fundamental period $N_0$ of $x[n]=\\cos\\!\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{9}n+\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)$.',
  opts:['$N_0=9$ samples',
        '$N_0=9/2$ samples',
        '$N_0=18$ samples',
        'Aperiodic, because $\\omega_0$ is not an integer multiple of $2\\pi$'],
  a:0,
  why:'$N=(2\\pi/\\omega_0)k=(9/2)k$ is an integer first at $k=2$, giving $N_0=9$; direct substitution confirms $\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}(n+9)+\\frac{\\pi}{5}\\right)=\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}n+\\frac{\\pi}{5}+4\\pi\\right)$.',
  wrong:{1:'$9/2$ comes from taking $k=1$ and ignoring the requirement that a discrete-time period must be an integer number of samples.',
         2:'$18$ is a period ($k=4$) but not the fundamental one; $N_0$ is by definition the smallest positive integer that works, and $9$ already does.',
         3:'The test is whether $\\omega_0/2\\pi$ is rational, not whether $\\omega_0$ is a multiple of $2\\pi$. Here $\\omega_0/2\\pi=2/9\\in\\mathbb{Q}$, so the signal is periodic.'},
  hints:['Compute $2\\pi/\\omega_0$ first, then find the smallest integer $k$ that clears the denominator.',
         'The phase offset $\\pi/5$ shifts the sequence but cannot change its period.'],
  sol:'Governing condition : $N=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega_0}k$ must be a positive integer, and $N_0$ is the smallest such $N$.<br>Step 1 — identify $\\omega_0=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{9}$ rad/sample.<br>Step 2 — form the candidate periods:$$N=\\frac{2\\pi}{4\\pi/9}k=\\frac{9}{2}k,\\qquad k=1,2,3,\\dots$$Step 3 — the first integer value occurs at $k=2$, giving $N_0=9$ samples.<br>Step 4 — verify directly:$$\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}(n+9)+\\frac{\\pi}{5}\\right)=\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}n+\\frac{\\pi}{5}+4\\pi\\right)=\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}n+\\frac{\\pi}{5}\\right)\\;\\forall n,$$and no smaller positive integer works, since $9k/2$ is an integer only for even $k$.<br>Interpretation: the sequence completes exactly two full cycles ($k=2$) in nine samples, so the pattern of nine stem values repeats indefinitely. Equivalently $\\omega_0/2\\pi=2/9$: two cycles per nine samples.<br>Numerical check: evaluating the sequence for $n=-200\\dots200$ shows agreement with its 9-sample shift to machine precision, and disagreement for every $N<9$.',
  err:'Answering $N_0=9/2=4.5$ by applying the continuous-time formula $T_0=2\\pi/\\omega_0$ and never imposing integrality.',
  teach:'A correct answer must name the integer $k$ that was used; without it the student cannot demonstrate that the period found is the smallest one.' },

{ id:'Q1-07', module:'M1', kind:'misconception', src:'pp. 2–3',
  stem:'A student writes: "$x(t)=2u(t)$ has the constant value 2 for $t\\ge0$, so its energy is $E_\\infty=4$ J and it is an energy signal." Which statement is correct?',
  opts:['$E_\\infty\\to\\infty$ and $P_\\infty=2$ W: it is a power signal.',
        '$E_\\infty\\to\\infty$ and $P_\\infty=4$ W: it is a power signal.',
        'The student is right: $E_\\infty=4$ J and $P_\\infty=0$, an energy signal.',
        'Both $E_\\infty$ and $P_\\infty$ are infinite, so the signal is neither an energy nor a power signal.'],
  a:0,
  why:'The energy integral $\\int_{0}^{\\infty}4\\,dt$ diverges, while $P_\\infty=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{0}^{T}4\\,dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{4T}{2T}=2$ W, because the averaging window $[-T,T]$ is twice as long as the interval the signal occupies.',
  wrong:{1:'$4$ W would be the answer if the window were $[0,T]$. The course definition divides by the full window length $2T$, and the signal is zero on the left half of it, so the average is halved to 2 W.',
         2:'The number 4 is the squared amplitude — an instantaneous power in watts, not an energy. Multiplying it by an infinite duration gives infinite energy, so the signal cannot be an energy signal.',
         3:'$P_\\infty$ is finite: the ratio $4T/(2T)$ is constant at 2 for every $T$, so the limit exists and equals 2 W.'},
  hints:['Write out $\\int_{0}^{\\infty}|2u(t)|^{2}dt$ explicitly before deciding on the class.',
         'In $P_\\infty$ the numerator only accumulates over $[0,T]$, but the denominator is $2T$.'],
  sol:'Governing definitions. Note $|x(t)|^{2}=4$ for $t\\ge0$ and $0$ for $t<0$.<br>Energy:$$E_\\infty=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^{2}dt=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}4\\,dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}4T\\to\\infty,$$so the signal is not an energy signal — the constant 4 is a power density in watts, not an energy.<br>Power:$$P_\\infty=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^{2}dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{0}^{T}4\\,dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{4T}{2T}=2\\ \\text{W}.$$Classification: $P_\\infty=2$ W $<\\infty$ with $E_\\infty\\to\\infty$, hence a power signal.<br>Interpretation: the step delivers 4 W while it is on, but it is on for only half of the symmetric averaging window, so the long-run average is 2 W. Compare the definition example $x[n]=4\\;\\forall n$, which is on for the whole window and therefore has $P_\\infty=16$ W with no halving.<br>Cross-check: $\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{0}^{T}4\\,dt=2$ for every finite $T$, so the limit is 2 without any limiting subtlety.',
  err:'Treating the squared amplitude as an energy ("$2^{2}=4$ J"), which conflates a rate (W) with an accumulated quantity (J).',
  teach:'Watch specifically for the factor-of-two error in one-sided signals: a correct script shows the numerator integrated over $[0,T]$ against a denominator $2T$.' },

{ id:'Q1-08', module:'M1', kind:'misconception', src:'p. 4',
  stem:'Let $x(t)=1$ for $2\\le t\\le6$ and zero otherwise. Using the course procedure for $x(at-b)$ — shift first, then scale — determine the interval on which $y(t)=x(2t+6)$ is non-zero.',
  opts:['$-5\\le t\\le-3$',
        '$-2\\le t\\le 0$',
        '$-4\\le t\\le 0$',
        '$-8\\le t\\le 0$'],
  a:1,
  why:'With $a=2$ and $b=-6$: the shift $v(t)=x(t+6)$ occupies $[-4,0]$, and the scaling $y(t)=v(2t)$ compresses that interval by 2 to $[-2,0]$ — confirmed by solving $2\\le 2t+6\\le 6$ directly.',
  wrong:{0:'This is the scale-first answer: $w(t)=x(2t)$ occupies $[1,3]$, and $w(t+6)$ occupies $[-5,-3]$. But $w(t+6)=x\\bigl(2(t+6)\\bigr)=x(2t+12)$, which is a different signal from $x(2t+6)$.',
         2:'$[-4,0]$ is the intermediate result $v(t)=x(t+6)$; the compression by the factor $a=2$ has not been applied yet.',
         3:'$[-8,0]$ applies the scaling in the wrong direction — dividing the endpoints by $a$ would be $v(t/2)$, an expansion, whereas $a=2>1$ compresses.'},
  hints:['Write the transformation in the standard form $x(at-b)$ and read off $a$ and $b$ before doing anything else.',
         'As a check, solve the inequality $2\\le 2t+6\\le 6$ for $t$ directly.'],
  sol:'Governing procedure : for $y(t)=x(at-b)$, first shift, $v(t)=x(t-b)$, then scale, $y(t)=v(at)$.<br>Step 1 — identify the parameters. $x(2t+6)=x(2t-(-6))$, so $a=2$ and $b=-6$.<br>Step 2 — shift: $v(t)=x(t+6)$ is non-zero when $2\\le t+6\\le 6$, i.e. on $-4\\le t\\le 0$.<br>Step 3 — scale: $y(t)=v(2t)$ is non-zero when $-4\\le 2t\\le 0$, i.e. on$$-2\\le t\\le 0.$$Step 4 — independent verification without the two-step recipe:$$2\\le 2t+6\\le 6\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;-4\\le 2t\\le 0\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;-2\\le t\\le 0.$$Interpretation: the pulse is advanced and compressed. Its duration falls from 4 s to 2 s (compression by $a=2$, p. 4), and its centre moves from $t=4$ to $t=-1$.<br>Numerical check: sampling $x(2t+6)$ on a fine grid over $[-10,10]$ gives a first non-zero sample at $t=-2.0000$ and a last at $t=0.0000$; the scale-first sequence instead returns $[-5,-3]$, showing the two orders are genuinely different signals.',
  err:'Scaling before shifting, which silently replaces $x(at-b)$ by $x(a(t-b))=x(at-ab)$ — here shifting the pulse by an extra 3 s.',
  teach:'If a student prefers the scale-first route, it is only valid with the shift $b/a$; ask them to state that division explicitly rather than accept the answer.' },

{ id:'Q1-09', module:'M1', kind:'exam', src:'pp. 2–4',
  stem:'Let $x(t)=e^{-|t|}$ and define $y(t)=x(2t-4)$. Determine (i) the total energy $E_y$ and (ii) the instant at which $|y(t)|$ attains its maximum.',
  opts:['$E_y=0.5$ J, maximum at $t=2$ s',
        '$E_y=1$ J, maximum at $t=2$ s',
        '$E_y=2$ J, maximum at $t=2$ s',
        '$E_y=0.5$ J, maximum at $t=4$ s'],
  a:0,
  why:'$E_x=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-2|t|}dt=1$ J; the substitution $\\lambda=2t-4$ gives $E_y=E_x/2=0.5$ J, and the peak of $x$ at $\\lambda=0$ maps to $2t-4=0$, i.e. $t=2$ s.',
  wrong:{1:'Time shifting alone preserves energy, but compression by $a=2$ does not: the signal is squeezed into half the time, so its energy is halved.',
         2:'$2$ J would correspond to an expansion ($0<a<1$). Because $a=2>1$ the waveform is compressed and the energy is divided, not multiplied, by 2.',
         3:'$t=4$ solves $t-4=0$, i.e. it ignores the factor 2 inside the argument. The peak occurs where the argument of $x$ vanishes: $2t-4=0$.'},
  hints:['Energy under time scaling: substitute $\\lambda=at-b$ in $\\int|x(at-b)|^{2}dt$ and track the Jacobian $d\\lambda=a\\,dt$.',
         'The maximum of $x(\\cdot)$ occurs where its argument is zero; set $2t-4=0$.'],
  sol:'Definitions: $E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^{2}dt$ ; shift-then-scale.<br>Step 1 — energy of the original signal:$$E_x=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\left(e^{-|t|}\\right)^{2}dt=2\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-2t}dt=2\\cdot\\frac12=1\\ \\text{J}.$$Step 2 — energy after the transformation. With $\\lambda=2t-4$, $d\\lambda=2\\,dt$:$$E_y=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\left|x(2t-4)\\right|^{2}dt=\\frac12\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(\\lambda)|^{2}d\\lambda=\\frac{E_x}{2}=0.5\\ \\text{J}.$$The shift contributes nothing, because the limits remain $\\pm\\infty$; only the factor $a=2$ matters.<br>Step 3 — location of the peak. $x(\\lambda)=e^{-|\\lambda|}$ is maximal at $\\lambda=0$, so $2t-4=0$ gives $t=2$ s, where $y(2)=1$.<br>Interpretation: $y$ is the same pulse advanced and compressed — half as wide, same height, hence half the energy. Height is unchanged, so the energy reduction comes entirely from the reduced duration.<br>Verification: symbolic integration of $e^{-2|2t-4|}$ over $\\mathbb{R}$ returns exactly $1/2$, and a fine-grid search for the maximum of $e^{-|2t-4|}$ returns $t=2.0000$.',
  err:'Assuming that energy is invariant under all of the basic operations; shifting and reversal preserve energy, but scaling by $a$ divides it by $|a|$.',
  teach:'Require the substitution and its Jacobian to be shown; an answer of $0.5$ J without $d\\lambda=2\\,dt$ may well be a guess.' },

{ id:'Q1-10', module:'M1', kind:'exam', src:'pp. 5, 8',
  stem:'For $x(t)=4\\cos(6t)+2\\sin(9t)$ with $t$ in seconds, determine the fundamental period $T_0$ and the fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$.',
  opts:['$T_0=2\\pi/3$ s and $\\omega_0=3$ rad/s',
        '$T_0=\\pi/3$ s and $\\omega_0=6$ rad/s',
        '$T_0=2\\pi/15$ s and $\\omega_0=15$ rad/s',
        'The signal is aperiodic, because $9/6$ is not an integer'],
  a:0,
  why:'The two components have $T_1=\\pi/3$ s and $T_2=2\\pi/9$ s; the smallest common multiple is $2T_1=3T_2=2\\pi/3$ s, so $T_0=2\\pi/3$ s and $\\omega_0=2\\pi/T_0=3$ rad/s.',
  wrong:{1:'$\\pi/3$ s repeats the $4\\cos(6t)$ term only: $\\sin\\!\\left(9(t+\\pi/3)\\right)=\\sin(9t+3\\pi)=-\\sin(9t)$, so the sum does not repeat.',
         2:'Frequencies of separate components are never added. $15$ rad/s corresponds to $6+9$, which has no periodicity meaning here.',
         3:'Periodicity of a sum requires the ratio of the two periods to be rational, not that one frequency divides the other. Here $T_1/T_2=3/2\\in\\mathbb{Q}$, so the sum is periodic.'},
  hints:['Find the fundamental period of each term separately using $T=2\\pi/\\omega$.',
         'Look for the smallest $T$ such that $T=m\\,T_1=n\\,T_2$ with integers $m$ and $n$.'],
  sol:'Definitions (pp. 5, 8): $T_0$ is the smallest $T>0$ with $x(t)=x(t+T)$ for all $t$, and $\\omega_0=2\\pi/T_0$.<br>Step 1 — component periods:$$T_1=\\frac{2\\pi}{6}=\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\ \\text{s},\\qquad T_2=\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\ \\text{s}.$$Step 2 — test commensurability: $\\dfrac{T_1}{T_2}=\\dfrac{\\pi/3}{2\\pi/9}=\\dfrac{3}{2}\\in\\mathbb{Q}$, so a common period exists and the sum is periodic.<br>Step 3 — smallest common multiple. Require $mT_1=nT_2$, i.e. $m\\frac{\\pi}{3}=n\\frac{2\\pi}{9}$, hence $3m=2n$. The smallest positive solution is $m=2$, $n=3$, giving$$T_0=2T_1=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\ \\text{s}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\omega_0=\\frac{2\\pi}{T_0}=3\\ \\text{rad/s}.$$Step 4 — direct verification:$$\\cos\\!\\left(6\\left(t+\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)=\\cos(6t+4\\pi)=\\cos6t,\\qquad \\sin\\!\\left(9\\left(t+\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)=\\sin(9t+6\\pi)=\\sin9t.$$Interpretation: $6$ and $9$ rad/s are the second and third harmonics of $\\omega_0=3$ rad/s — the fundamental frequency is their greatest common divisor, even though no component oscillates at 3 rad/s.<br>Numerical check: scanning candidate periods in steps of $\\pi/12$ over $(0,2\\pi]$ identifies $T=2.0944$ s $=2\\pi/3$ as the smallest value for which the waveform matches its shifted copy to $10^{-9}$.',
  err:'Reporting the period of the slowest component ($\\pi/3$ s from the 6 rad/s term) without checking that the second component also repeats over the same interval.',
  teach:'Ask for the integer pair $(m,n)$; students who cannot supply it usually guessed the least common multiple rather than deriving it.' },

{ id:'Q1-11', module:'M1', kind:'graph', src:'pp. 3–4',
  stem:'A discrete-time signal is displayed as a stem plot with exactly four non-zero stems: height $2$ at $n=-3$, height $-1$ at $n=-2$, height $3$ at $n=0$ and height $1$ at $n=1$. Which stem plot represents $y[n]=x[-n+2]$?',
  opts:['Heights $1$ at $n=1$, $3$ at $n=2$, $-1$ at $n=4$, $2$ at $n=5$.',
        'Heights $2$ at $n=-5$, $-1$ at $n=-4$, $3$ at $n=-2$, $1$ at $n=-1$.',
        'Heights $1$ at $n=-3$, $3$ at $n=-2$, $-1$ at $n=0$, $2$ at $n=1$.',
        'Heights $1$ at $n=-1$, $3$ at $n=0$, $-1$ at $n=2$, $2$ at $n=3$.'],
  a:0,
  why:'$y[n]=x[2-n]$ is non-zero exactly when $2-n\\in\\{-3,-2,0,1\\}$, i.e. at $n=5,4,2,1$, carrying the values $x[-3]=2$, $x[-2]=-1$, $x[0]=3$ and $x[1]=1$ respectively.',
  wrong:{1:'These are the stems of $x[n+2]$: the advance was applied but the reflection was not.',
         2:'This is $x[-n-2]$, obtained by reflecting and then advancing by 2. Reflecting the shifted variable changes the sign of the shift, which is precisely the trap in this transformation.',
         3:'This is the pure reflection $x[-n]$, with the shift by 2 omitted altogether.'},
  hints:['Write the transformation as $y[n]=x[an-b]$ with $a=-1$; identify $b$ before sketching anything.',
         'A safe check: pick one sample, e.g. $n=5$, and evaluate $x[2-5]=x[-3]$.'],
  sol:'Governing operations (pp. 3–4): reflection $x[-n]$ and shift $x[n-n_0]$, combined by the shift-then-scale rule.<br>Step 1 — normal form. $y[n]=x[-n+2]=x[(-1)n-(-2)]$, so $a=-1$ and $b=-2$.<br>Step 2 — shift first: $v[n]=x[n+2]$, whose stems move two positions left, to $n=-5,-4,-2,-1$ with heights $2,-1,3,1$.<br>Step 3 — scale (here reflect): $y[n]=v[-n]$ mirrors those positions about $n=0$, giving stems at $n=5,4,2,1$ with heights $2,-1,3,1$ respectively.<br>Step 4 — sample-by-sample confirmation:$$y[1]=x[1]=1,\\quad y[2]=x[0]=3,\\quad y[4]=x[-2]=-1,\\quad y[5]=x[-3]=2,$$and $y[n]=0$ for every other $n$.<br>Interpretation: the plot is the original stem pattern read backwards and relocated so that the sample originally at $n=0$ now sits at $n=2$. The reflection maps the support $[-3,1]$ to $[1,5]$, preserving its length of five sample positions.<br>Verification: enumerating $y[n]=x[2-n]$ for $n=-10\\dots10$ reproduces exactly the set of pairs $(1,1),(2,3),(4,-1),(5,2)$, and each of the three distractors corresponds to a different, distinct transformation.',
  err:'Reflecting first and then shifting in the same direction as the printed sign, which produces $x[-n-2]$ and moves the pattern to the negative axis.',
  teach:'The sign of the shift after a reflection is the discriminating step; check the student\'s intermediate signal $v[n]$ rather than only the final sketch.' },

{ id:'Q1-12', module:'M1', kind:'synthesis', src:'p. 2',
  stem:'A piezoelectric shock sensor produces the voltage burst $v(t)=6e^{-500t}u(t)$ V across a matched load of $R=50\\,\\Omega$. Using $p(t)=v^{2}(t)/R$, compute the actual energy delivered to the load.',
  opts:['$0.72$ mJ',
        '$36$ mJ',
        '$1.44$ mJ',
        '$1.8$ J'],
  a:0,
  why:'$\\int_{0}^{\\infty}v^{2}(t)dt=36/1000=0.036$ V$^{2}$s, and dividing by $R=50\\,\\Omega$ gives $7.2\\times10^{-4}$ J $=0.72$ mJ.',
  wrong:{1:'$36$ mJ is the normalised ($R=1\\,\\Omega$) energy $0.036$ V$^{2}$s quoted as if it were in joules. It must still be divided by the actual load resistance.',
         2:'$1.44$ mJ follows from $\\int 36e^{-500t}dt/50$ — the amplitude was squared but the exponent was not, so the decay rate was left at 500 instead of 1000 s$^{-1}$.',
         3:'$1.8$ J multiplies by $R$ instead of dividing. The instantaneous power is $v^{2}/R$, so a larger load resistance absorbs less energy from a given voltage waveform.'},
  hints:['Square the whole waveform first, then integrate, then divide by $R$ once.',
         '$\\int_{0}^{\\infty}A^{2}e^{-2at}dt=A^{2}/(2a)$; here $A=6$ V and $a=500$ s$^{-1}$.'],
  sol:'Governing relations : $p(t)=v(t)i(t)=v^{2}(t)/R$ and $E=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}p(t)\\,dt$.<br>Step 1 — squared waveform: $v^{2}(t)=36e^{-1000t}$ for $t\\ge0$ (the decay rate doubles under squaring).<br>Step 2 — integrate:$$\\int_{0}^{\\infty}36e^{-1000t}\\,dt=\\frac{36}{1000}=0.036\\ \\mathrm{V^{2}\\,s}.$$This is exactly the normalised energy $E_\\infty$ of the course notes, i.e. the value obtained with $R=1\\,\\Omega$.<br>Step 3 — restore the physical resistance:$$E=\\frac{1}{R}\\int_{0}^{\\infty}v^{2}(t)\\,dt=\\frac{0.036}{50}=7.2\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{J}=0.72\\ \\mathrm{mJ}.$$Interpretation: the burst has a time constant of $1/500=2$ ms and a peak power of $6^{2}/50=0.72$ W, so an energy of well under a millijoule is expected; indeed $0.72$ W times the effective duration of 1 ms gives the same order.<br>Engineering note: the notes move from $(1/R)v^{2}(t)$ to $|x(t)|^{2}$ by silently setting $R=1\\,\\Omega$. That normalisation is harmless when signals are compared with each other, but any answer in joules for a real load must carry the $1/R$ factor, as here.<br>Cross-check: peak power $\\times$ half the time constant $=0.72\\times10^{-3}$ W$\\cdot$s, matching the exact result $A^{2}/(2aR)=36/(1000\\cdot50)$.',
  err:'Reporting the normalised energy $0.036$ J as the delivered energy, i.e. forgetting that the course convention silently sets $R=1\\,\\Omega$.',
  teach:'This question exists to expose the $R=1$ convention; require the units V$^{2}$s on the intermediate integral and J only after dividing by $R$.' },

/* ============= MODULE 2 — SYSTEMS AND THEIR PROPERTIES (pp. 11–14) ============= */

{ id:'Q2-01', module:'M2', kind:'concept', src:'p. 11',
  stem:'Which of the following systems is memoryless?',
  opts:['$y[n]=x[n]-x[n-2]$',
        '$y(t)=e^{x(t)}\\cos(2t)$',
        '$y(t)=\\displaystyle\\int_{t-1}^{t}x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$',
        '$y[n]=x[n]+y[n-1]$'],
  a:1,
  why:'Memorylessness asks only whether the output at time $t$ is determined by the input at that same instant. The factor $\\cos(2t)$ is a known deterministic function of $t$, not a stored input value, so $y(t)$ depends on $x$ only through $x(t)$.',
  wrong:{0:'The sample $x[n-2]$ must be retained for two steps, so the system stores past input values.',
         2:'The integral accumulates the input over the whole window $[t-1,t]$; a one-second history must be stored.',
         3:'Back-substitution gives $y[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}x[n-k]$, so the output depends on the entire input history.'},
  hints:['Ask only one question: to compute $y$ at a given instant, do I need any value of $x$ at a different instant?',
         'An explicit dependence on $t$ or $n$ in a coefficient is not memory — memory is a dependence on the input at other times.'],
  sol:'Definition : a system is memoryless if the output at time $t$ (or $n$) depends only on the input at that same time.<br>Option A: computing $y[n]$ requires $x[n-2]$, a value from two steps earlier — memory.<br>Option B: fixing $t$ determines $\\cos(2t)$ as a number, and then $y(t)=e^{x(t)}\\cos(2t)$ is a fixed function of the single value $x(t)$. No other input value is needed, so the system is memoryless. This mirrors the definition example $y(t)=[2x(t)-x^{2}(t)]^{2}$, which is memoryless although non-linear, and $y(t)=x(t)\\cos(t+1)$, where the explicit $t$ is a known gain.<br>Option C: the integral needs every $x(\\tau)$ with $t-1\\le\\tau\\le t$ — memory of one second.<br>Option D: the recursion accumulates all past inputs, $y[n]=\\sum_{k\\ge0}x[n-k]$ — infinite memory.<br>Interpretation: memorylessness is about the time argument of $x$, not about the algebraic complexity of the rule. A memoryless system may be non-linear (option B is), time varying (option B is), or both.<br>Cross-check: a resistor $v(t)=Ri(t)$ is memoryless, whereas a capacitor $v(t)=\\frac{1}{C}\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}i(\\tau)d\\tau$ is not  — exactly the contrast between B and C.',
  err:'Declaring option B to have memory because $\\cos(2t)$ "changes with time"; a time-varying coefficient makes the system time varying, not a system with memory.',
  teach:'Separate the two ideas explicitly when marking: memory concerns the time argument of the input, time variance concerns an explicit $t$ in the rule.' },

{ id:'Q2-02', module:'M2', kind:'concept', src:'pp. 11–12',
  stem:'Which argument correctly establishes that a system is not invertible?',
  opts:['Show that the system has memory, since a system that stores past inputs cannot be one-to-one.',
        'Exhibit two distinct input signals that produce identical outputs for all time.',
        'Show that the system is non-linear, because only linear systems possess inverses.',
        'Show that at some instant the output is smaller in magnitude than the input, so information has been lost.'],
  a:1,
  why:'Invertibility means the input-to-output map is one-to-one ; a single pair of distinct inputs sharing one output destroys that property, exactly as $x_1(t)=1$ and $x_2(t)=-1$ do for $y(t)=x^{2}(t)$.',
  wrong:{0:'Memory is irrelevant to invertibility. The unit delay $y[n]=x[n-1]$ has memory yet is invertible via $x[n]=y[n+1]$, and the accumulator is invertible via the first difference.',
         2:'Non-linearity does not preclude an inverse: $y(t)=x^{3}(t)$ is non-linear and invertible with $x(t)=y^{1/3}(t)$. Conversely, the linear system $y[n]=n\\,x[n]$ is not invertible, because $x[0]$ is lost.',
         3:'A gain of less than one loses no information: $y(t)=0.1\\,x(t)$ is invertible via $x(t)=10\\,y(t)$. What matters is whether two different inputs can collide, not the size of the output.'},
  hints:['State the definition first: distinct inputs must give distinct outputs.',
         'To disprove a one-to-one property you need a collision — two inputs, one output.'],
  sol:'Definition : a system is invertible when distinct inputs produce distinct outputs, so that the input can be recovered from the output. The notes give two routes: (1) exhibit an inversion formula to prove invertibility, (2) exhibit a counterexample to disprove it.<br>Disproof structure: find $x_1\\neq x_2$ with $S\\{x_1\\}=S\\{x_2\\}$ for all time. Example : for $y(t)=x^{2}(t)$, take $x_1(t)=1$ and $x_2(t)=-1$; both give $y(t)=1$, so the sign of the input cannot be recovered.<br>Proof structure for the positive case : for $y(t)=[\\cos(t)+2]x(t)$, note $\\cos t+2\\ge1>0$, so the gain never vanishes and$$x(t)=\\frac{y(t)}{\\cos(t)+2}$$is a valid inversion formula, which settles the question affirmatively.<br>Interpretation: invertibility is a statement about the map as a whole. Neither memory, nor linearity, nor the amplitude of the output enters the definition.<br>Sanity check on the distractors: delay (memory, invertible), cube law (non-linear, invertible), $0.1\\,x(t)$ (attenuating, invertible) — each is a counterexample to one of the three rejected criteria.',
  err:'Arguing informally that "information is lost" without exhibiting two concrete inputs that collide.',
  teach:'Award credit only for an explicit counterexample or an explicit inversion formula; verbal plausibility arguments should not pass here.' },

{ id:'Q2-03', module:'M2', kind:'concept', src:'p. 12',
  stem:'Which statement expresses the BIBO stability requirement correctly?',
  opts:['There must exist at least one bounded input whose response is bounded.',
        'The output must remain bounded for every input, whether that input is bounded or not.',
        'Every input satisfying $|x(t)|<B<\\infty$ for all $t$ must produce a bounded output; a single bounded input with an unbounded response disproves stability.',
        'The output magnitude must never exceed the input magnitude at any instant.'],
  a:2,
  why:'BIBO stability is a universal statement over all bounded inputs, so it is proved by a bound valid for every bounded input and disproved by one counterexample, exactly as the accumulator is disproved by $x[n]=u[n]$.',
  wrong:{0:'An existential statement is far too weak: the zero input always produces a bounded output, so every system would qualify.',
         1:'Unbounded inputs are excluded by definition. Requiring boundedness for them would disqualify even the identity system, since $y=x$ is unbounded when $x$ is.',
         3:'That describes a passivity or gain-limiting property, not stability. The amplifier $y(t)=100\\,x(t)$ magnifies every input yet is perfectly BIBO stable, since $|y|\\le100B<\\infty$.'},
  hints:['Note the two quantifiers: "for every bounded input" and "there exists a finite bound on the output".',
         'How would you disprove a statement that begins with "for every"?'],
  sol:'Definition : a signal is bounded if there exists $B<\\infty$ with $|x(t)|<B$ for all $t$; the system is stable if every bounded input produces a bounded output.<br>Proof pattern (source example, p. 12): for $y(t)=2x^{2}(t-1)+x(3t)$ and any $|x|\\le B$,$$|y(t)|\\le 2|x(t-1)|^{2}+|x(3t)|\\le 2B^{2}+B<\\infty,$$a bound holding for every bounded input, so the system is stable.<br>Disproof pattern : for the accumulator $y[n]=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{n}x[k]$, the bounded input $x[n]=u[n]$ (with $B=1$) gives $y[n]=n+1\\to\\infty$. One counterexample is enough.<br>Interpretation: stability constrains the map only on the set of bounded inputs, and it says nothing about how large the output bound may be — a large but finite gain is still stable.<br>Verification of the wording: the accepted statement contains both the universal condition and the falsification rule, which is what the two proof patterns above require.',
  err:'Testing stability with one convenient input, finding a bounded output, and concluding stability — that only fails to disprove it.',
  teach:'When marking, check the direction of the argument: a general inequality for a proof, a single explicit input for a disproof.' },

{ id:'Q2-04', module:'M2', kind:'calc', src:'pp. 4, 13',
  stem:'Apply the formal time-invariance test to $y(t)=x(2t)$ with $t_0=3$ s. Let $y_1(t)$ be the response to $x_1(t)$, and let $y_2(t)$ be the response to the shifted input $x_2(t)=x_1(t-3)$. Which pair of expressions is correct?',
  opts:['$y_2(t)=x_1(2t-6)$ and $y_1(t-3)=x_1(2t-3)$; the system is not time invariant.',
        '$y_2(t)=x_1(2t-3)$ and $y_1(t-3)=x_1(2t-6)$; the system is not time invariant.',
        '$y_2(t)=x_1(2t-3)$ and $y_1(t-3)=x_1(2t-3)$; the system is time invariant.',
        '$y_2(t)=x_1(2t-6)$ and $y_1(t-3)=x_1(2t-6)$; the system is time invariant.'],
  a:1,
  why:'Feeding $x_2$ through the rule replaces the argument $t$ by $2t$ inside $x_2$, giving $y_2(t)=x_2(2t)=x_1(2t-3)$, whereas shifting the output replaces $t$ by $t-3$ in $x_1(2t)$, giving $y_1(t-3)=x_1(2t-6)$. The two differ, so the system is not time invariant.',
  wrong:{0:'The two expressions are interchanged. Shifting the input delays it by 3 s before the compression, which appears as $-3$ inside the argument; shifting the output delays an already compressed signal, which appears as $-6$.',
         2:'$y_1(t-3)$ is obtained by substituting $t\\to t-3$ everywhere in $y_1(t)=x_1(2t)$, which gives $x_1(2(t-3))=x_1(2t-6)$, not $x_1(2t-3)$.',
         3:'$y_2$ is computed by substituting the shifted input into the rule, i.e. $x_2(2t)=x_1(2t-3)$. Writing $x_1(2t-6)$ here confuses the input shift with the output shift.'},
  hints:['Compute $y_2(t)=x_2(2t)$ by substituting the definition of $x_2$ after the scaling, not before.',
         'Compute $y_1(t-3)$ by replacing every $t$ in $y_1(t)=x_1(2t)$ with $t-3$.'],
  sol:'Test : the system is time invariant if and only if $y_2(t)=y_1(t-t_0)$ for every $t_0$ and every input.<br>Step 1 — response to the shifted input. The rule maps any input $v$ to $v(2t)$. With $v=x_2$,$$y_2(t)=x_2(2t)=x_1(2t-3).$$Step 2 — shifted response. From $y_1(t)=x_1(2t)$,$$y_1(t-3)=x_1\\bigl(2(t-3)\\bigr)=x_1(2t-6).$$Step 3 — compare. $x_1(2t-3)\\neq x_1(2t-6)$ in general, so the system is not time invariant. The two agree only for $t_0=0$.<br>Concrete confirmation: take $x_1(t)=e^{-(t-1)^{2}}$. Then $y_2$ peaks where $2t-3=1$, i.e. $t=2$, while $y_1(t-3)$ peaks where $2t-6=1$, i.e. $t=3.5$. A numerical comparison on a fine grid over $[-5,5]$ shows the two waveforms are not equal.<br>Interpretation: compressing by 2 halves every delay. A 3-second delay at the input emerges as a 1.5-second delay at the output, so shift and scale do not commute — the same asymmetry that forces the shift-then-scale ordering of p. 4.<br>Cross-check with the definition: the analogous decimator $y[n]=(x[2n])^{2}$ is listed as not time invariant  for exactly this reason.',
  err:'Substituting $t\\to t-3$ into the input argument only ($x_1(2t-3)$ for both sides), which quietly assumes the very property under test.',
  teach:'Insist on two separately derived expressions before any comparison; a single line of algebra usually means one of the two was assumed.' },

{ id:'Q2-05', module:'M2', kind:'calc', src:'p. 14',
  stem:'Test $y[n]=x[n]\\,x[n-1]$ for linearity using $x_1[n]=\\delta[n]$, $x_2[n]=\\delta[n-1]$ and the coefficients $a=b=1$. Evaluate the true response $y_3[n]$ to $x_1[n]+x_2[n]$ at $n=1$ and compare it with $y_1[1]+y_2[1]$.',
  opts:['$y_3[1]=1$ while $y_1[1]+y_2[1]=0$: the system is not linear.',
        '$y_3[1]=0$ and $y_1[1]+y_2[1]=0$: the test is passed, so the system is linear.',
        '$y_3[1]=1$ and $y_1[1]+y_2[1]=1$: superposition holds at $n=1$, so the system is linear.',
        '$y_3[1]=2$ while $y_1[1]+y_2[1]=1$: the system is not linear.'],
  a:0,
  why:'$y_1[n]=\\delta[n]\\delta[n-1]=0$ and $y_2[n]=\\delta[n-1]\\delta[n-2]=0$ for all $n$, but the sum input gives $y_3[1]=(\\delta[1]+\\delta[0])(\\delta[0]+\\delta[-1])=1\\cdot1=1\\neq0$.',
  wrong:{1:'$y_3[1]$ is not zero. At $n=1$ the first factor $x_3[1]=\\delta[1]+\\delta[0]=0+1=1$ and the second factor $x_3[0]=\\delta[0]+\\delta[-1]=1+0=1$, so the product is 1.',
         2:'$y_1[1]+y_2[1]=0$, not 1: each individual response vanishes identically, because in each case one of the two factors is always zero.',
         3:'$y_3[1]=1$, not 2 — the two factors are each equal to 1 and are multiplied, not added; and $y_1[1]+y_2[1]$ is 0, not 1.'},
  hints:['Evaluate $y_1[n]$ and $y_2[n]$ for all $n$ first — each is identically zero.',
         'Write $x_3[n]=\\delta[n]+\\delta[n-1]$ and evaluate $x_3[1]x_3[0]$ directly.'],
  sol:'Definition : the system is linear if $a x_1+b x_2\\to a y_1+b y_2$ for all inputs and all $a,b\\in\\mathbb{C}$.<br>Step 1 — individual responses.$$y_1[n]=\\delta[n]\\,\\delta[n-1]=0\\ \\forall n,\\qquad y_2[n]=\\delta[n-1]\\,\\delta[n-2]=0\\ \\forall n,$$because the two impulses in each product never occupy the same index. Hence $y_1[1]+y_2[1]=0$.<br>Step 2 — response to the sum. With $x_3[n]=\\delta[n]+\\delta[n-1]$,$$y_3[n]=x_3[n]\\,x_3[n-1]=\\bigl(\\delta[n]+\\delta[n-1]\\bigr)\\bigl(\\delta[n-1]+\\delta[n-2]\\bigr).$$At $n=1$: $x_3[1]=1$ and $x_3[0]=1$, so $y_3[1]=1$.<br>Step 3 — compare: $y_3[1]=1\\neq0=y_1[1]+y_2[1]$, so additivity fails and the system is not linear.<br>Interpretation: the cross term $x_1[n]x_2[n-1]+x_2[n]x_1[n-1]$ is what superposition cannot reproduce — structurally the same defect as the cross term $2ab\\,x_1[2n]x_2[2n]$  example $y[n]=(x[2n])^{2}$.<br>Numerical verification: forming the three sequences on $n=-3\\dots7$ gives $y_1\\equiv0$, $y_2\\equiv0$ and $y_3[1]=1$ with all other samples zero, confirming the single point of failure.',
  err:'Concluding linearity from $y_1\\equiv0$ and $y_2\\equiv0$ without ever computing the response to the sum.',
  teach:'A valid disproof needs all three responses; if the script contains only two, the decisive comparison was never made.' },

{ id:'Q2-06', module:'M2', kind:'calc', src:'p. 12',
  stem:'For the system $y(t)=3x(t-2)+2x^{2}(t)$ all admissible inputs satisfy $|x(t)|\\le4$ for every $t$. Using the triangle inequality, what bound $M$ with $|y(t)|\\le M$ does this argument give, and is it attained?',
  opts:['$M=20$, attained by the constant input $x(t)=4$.',
        '$M=44$, attained by the constant input $x(t)=4$.',
        '$M=76$, attained by the constant input $x(t)=4$.',
        '$M=32$; the first term cannot contribute, because it is evaluated at $t-2$.'],
  a:1,
  why:'$|y(t)|\\le3|x(t-2)|+2|x(t)|^{2}\\le3(4)+2(16)=44$, and the constant input $x(t)=4$ gives exactly $y(t)=12+32=44$, so the bound is tight.',
  wrong:{0:'$20=3(4)+2(4)$ omits the squaring in the second term; $2x^{2}$ evaluated at $|x|=4$ contributes $32$, not $8$.',
         2:'$76=3(4)+(2\\cdot4)^{2}$ squares the coefficient as well. The term is $2\\,x^{2}(t)$, i.e. the square is taken first and then multiplied by 2.',
         3:'A delayed input is still an input: $|x(t-2)|\\le4$ holds for every $t$, so the first term contributes up to 12 and cannot be dropped.'},
  hints:['Apply $|p+q|\\le|p|+|q|$ and then bound each term separately.',
         'Check attainability by trying the simplest bounded input, a constant at the limit value.'],
  sol:'Definitions : $|x(t)|\\le B$ for all $t$ with $B=4$; BIBO stability requires a finite bound on $|y|$.<br>Step 1 — triangle inequality:$$|y(t)|=\\bigl|3x(t-2)+2x^{2}(t)\\bigr|\\le3\\,|x(t-2)|+2\\,|x(t)|^{2}.$$Step 2 — insert the bound. Since the constraint $|x|\\le4$ holds at every instant, it holds at $t-2$ as well:$$|y(t)|\\le3(4)+2(4)^{2}=12+32=44.$$Step 3 — attainability. Take $x(t)=4$ for all $t$. Then $y(t)=3(4)+2(16)=44$ exactly, so no smaller bound is possible for this input set and $M=44$ is tight.<br>Conclusion: the system is BIBO stable, since every bounded input yields $|y|\\le3B+2B^{2}<\\infty$ — the same argument the notes use for $y(t)=2x^{2}(t-1)+x(3t)$, where the bound is $2B^{2}+B$.<br>Units and interpretation: if $x$ is in volts, the quadratic term dominates for large $B$; the bound grows like $2B^{2}$, so stability holds for every finite $B$ even though the gain is not uniform.<br>Cross-check: evaluating $3x(t-2)+2x^{2}(t)$ for the constant inputs $x=\\pm4$ gives $44$ and $-12+32=20$ respectively, confirming that $44$ is the maximum over that family.',
  err:'Bounding $2x^{2}(t)$ by $2B=8$ instead of $2B^{2}=32$, i.e. applying the bound before the non-linearity.',
  teach:'Look for the order of operations in the bounding step; the square must be taken on the bound, not the bound on the square.' },

{ id:'Q2-07', module:'M2', kind:'misconception', src:'pp. 11, 14',
  stem:'A student argues: "$y[n]=5x[n]-2$ uses only the present sample, so it is memoryless; and a memoryless system is automatically linear." Which statement is correct?',
  opts:['The system is memoryless and linear; the constant $-2$ merely shifts the output level.',
        'The system is memoryless, but it is not linear: with $a=b=1$ the true response to $x_1[n]+x_2[n]$ exceeds $y_1[n]+y_2[n]$ by 2 at every $n$.',
        'The system has memory because of the constant term, and systems with memory are never linear.',
        'The system is linear but time varying, because a constant is added at every instant.'],
  a:1,
  why:'Memorylessness and linearity are independent properties. Here the true response is $5(x_1+x_2)-2$ while superposition predicts $5(x_1+x_2)-4$, a discrepancy of exactly $+2$, so additivity fails.',
  wrong:{0:'An output offset destroys linearity: linearity requires the zero input to give the zero output, whereas $x[n]=0$ gives $y[n]=-2$. Homogeneity fails too, since scaling the input by $a$ does not scale $-2$.',
         2:'A constant term is not memory; nothing from a previous instant is stored. Moreover, systems with memory can be perfectly linear — the accumulator and the unit delay both are.',
         3:'The rule contains no explicit $n$, so it is time invariant: delaying the input delays the output identically. What it fails is linearity, not time invariance.'},
  hints:['Test the zero input first: what must a linear system produce from $x[n]=0$?',
         'Write out $S\\{x_1+x_2\\}$ and $S\\{x_1\\}+S\\{x_2\\}$ side by side and subtract.'],
  sol:'Definitions (pp. 11, 14): memoryless — the output at $n$ depends only on $x[n]$; linear — $ax_1+bx_2\\to ay_1+by_2$ for all $a,b\\in\\mathbb{C}$.<br>Memorylessness: computing $y[n]$ requires only $x[n]$, so the first half of the student\'s claim is correct.<br>Linearity test with $a=b=1$:$$S\\{x_1+x_2\\}[n]=5\\bigl(x_1[n]+x_2[n]\\bigr)-2,\\qquad y_1[n]+y_2[n]=5x_1[n]-2+5x_2[n]-2=5\\bigl(x_1[n]+x_2[n]\\bigr)-4.$$The difference is $+2$ for every $n$ and every pair of inputs, so additivity fails and the system is not linear.<br>Homogeneity check: with $a=3$, $S\\{3x\\}=15x-2$ while $3y=15x-6$ — again unequal.<br>Zero-input test: a linear system must map $x\\equiv0$ to $y\\equiv0$; here $y\\equiv-2$, which settles the matter in one line.<br>Interpretation: this is an incrementally linear system — linear in the increments about the operating point, but not linear in the course sense. Full property list: memoryless, causal, time invariant, BIBO stable ($|y|\\le5B+2$), invertible ($x[n]=(y[n]+2)/5$), not linear.<br>Cross-check: the definition\'s linear example $y(t)=2\\pi x(t)$  has no additive constant, which is exactly what makes its proof of linearity go through.',
  err:'Believing that memoryless implies linear; there is the counterexample $y(t)=[2x(t)-x^{2}(t)]^{2}$, which is memoryless and non-linear.',
  teach:'The one-line zero-input test is the fastest marking check; accept it as a complete disproof of linearity.' },

{ id:'Q2-08', module:'M2', kind:'misconception', src:'p. 13',
  stem:'To test $y[n]=(-1)^{n}x[n]$ for time invariance, a student applies $x_1[n]=\\delta[n]$, then $x_2[n]=\\delta[n-2]$, observes that $y_2[n]=y_1[n-2]$, and concludes that the system is time invariant. Which statement is correct?',
  opts:['The conclusion is sound: verifying the property for one shift is enough, because time invariance is a linear property.',
        'The conclusion is wrong because $\\delta[n]$ may never be used as a test input for time invariance.',
        'The conclusion is unjustified: the test must hold for every $t_0$. With $t_0=1$, $y_2[n]=-\\delta[n-1]$ while $y_1[n-1]=\\delta[n-1]$, so the system is not time invariant.',
        'The conclusion is wrong because $(-1)^{n}$ makes the system non-linear, and time invariance cannot be discussed for non-linear systems.'],
  a:2,
  why:'Time invariance is a universal statement over all shifts. The shift $t_0=2$ happens to succeed because $(-1)^{n}$ has period 2, but $t_0=1$ produces a sign flip and breaks the requirement.',
  wrong:{0:'Time invariance quantifies over every $t_0$, so verifying one shift can never establish it. The special shift chosen here is a multiple of the period of $(-1)^{n}$, which is exactly why it passes.',
         1:'The impulse is a legitimate and convenient test input; the flaw is testing a single shift, not the choice of input.',
         3:'The system is linear — multiplication by the fixed sequence $(-1)^{n}$ preserves additivity and homogeneity, just as $y[n]=n\\,x[n]$ does. Time invariance is defined for arbitrary systems in any case.'},
  hints:['Write the test as a statement with a quantifier: for every $t_0$, $y_2[n]=y_1[n-t_0]$.',
         'Try the smallest non-trivial shift, $t_0=1$, and watch the sign of $(-1)^{n}$.'],
  sol:'Test : if $x[n]\\to y[n]$, then time invariance requires $x[n-n_0]\\to y[n-n_0]$ for every integer $n_0$.<br>Baseline: $x_1[n]=\\delta[n]$ gives $y_1[n]=(-1)^{n}\\delta[n]=\\delta[n]$.<br>Shift $n_0=2$: $x_2[n]=\\delta[n-2]$ gives $y_2[n]=(-1)^{n}\\delta[n-2]=(-1)^{2}\\delta[n-2]=\\delta[n-2]$, and $y_1[n-2]=\\delta[n-2]$. The test passes — but only because $(-1)^{n}$ repeats every two samples.<br>Shift $n_0=1$: $x_2[n]=\\delta[n-1]$ gives$$y_2[n]=(-1)^{n}\\delta[n-1]=(-1)^{1}\\delta[n-1]=-\\delta[n-1],$$while $y_1[n-1]=\\delta[n-1]$. Since $-\\delta[n-1]\\neq\\delta[n-1]$, the system is not time invariant.<br>General argument: for arbitrary input and shift, $y_2[n]=(-1)^{n}x_1[n-n_0]$ whereas $y_1[n-n_0]=(-1)^{n-n_0}x_1[n-n_0]$. These agree only when $(-1)^{n_0}=1$, i.e. for even $n_0$.<br>Interpretation: the system is periodically time varying with period 2. A single successful test is evidence of nothing; the definition disproves time invariance of $y[n]=n\\,x[n]$ with exactly one well-chosen counterexample, which is the legitimate direction of a single test.<br>Numerical verification: comparing the two sequences on $n=-6\\dots11$ confirms agreement for $n_0=2$ and disagreement for $n_0=1$.',
  err:'Treating one successful shift as a proof; a single test can only disprove a universal property, never establish it.',
  teach:'Reward a general symbolic comparison over any number of numeric spot checks — spot checks are only valid as counterexamples.' },

{ id:'Q2-09', module:'M2', kind:'exam', src:'pp. 11–14',
  stem:'Classify the continuous-time system $y(t)=x(t-2)+x(4-t)$ with respect to linearity, causality, BIBO stability and time invariance. Where boundedness is required, assume $|x(t)|\\le B<\\infty$.',
  opts:['Linear and BIBO stable, but neither causal nor time invariant.',
        'Linear, time invariant and BIBO stable, but not causal.',
        'Linear, causal and BIBO stable, but not time invariant.',
        'Linear and time invariant, but neither causal nor BIBO stable.'],
  a:0,
  why:'Both terms are linear in $x$ and each is bounded by $B$, giving $|y|\\le2B$; but $y(t)$ needs $x(4-t)$, a future value whenever $t<2$, and the reflected term breaks the shift test.',
  wrong:{1:'Time invariance fails. The response to $x_1(t-t_0)$ contains $x_1(4-t-t_0)$, whereas the shifted output contains $x_1(4-t+t_0)$; the sign of $t_0$ is reversed by the reflection.',
         2:'Causality fails. At $t=0$ the rule evaluates $x(4)$, an input four seconds in the future; the requirement is violated for every $t<2$.',
         3:'Stability holds: $|y(t)|\\le|x(t-2)|+|x(4-t)|\\le2B<\\infty$ for every bounded input, so the system cannot be unstable.'},
  hints:['Handle the two terms separately: a pure delay, and a reflection about $t=2$.',
         'For causality ask when $4-t>t$; for time invariance compare $x_1(4-t-t_0)$ with $x_1(4-t+t_0)$.'],
  sol:'Governing definitions.<br>Linearity: with $x=ax_1+bx_2$,$$y(t)=ax_1(t-2)+bx_2(t-2)+ax_1(4-t)+bx_2(4-t)=a\\,y_1(t)+b\\,y_2(t),$$so the system is linear (each term is a sample of the input, and sampling is linear).<br>BIBO stability: $|y(t)|\\le|x(t-2)|+|x(4-t)|\\le B+B=2B<\\infty$ for every bounded input, so the system is stable.<br>Causality: the second term needs the input at $\\tau=4-t$. This is a future instant whenever $4-t>t$, i.e. $t<2$; for example $y(0)=x(-2)+x(4)$ uses $x(4)$. Hence the system is not causal.<br>Time invariance: let $x_2(t)=x_1(t-t_0)$. Then$$y_2(t)=x_1(t-2-t_0)+x_1(4-t-t_0),\\qquad y_1(t-t_0)=x_1(t-t_0-2)+x_1\\bigl(4-(t-t_0)\\bigr)=x_1(4-t+t_0).$$The first terms agree, the second terms differ for every $t_0\\neq0$, so the system is not time invariant.<br>Interpretation: the reflection $x(4-t)$ is a mirror about $t=2$. Mirrors preserve linearity and boundedness but reverse the direction of time, which destroys both causality (for $t<2$) and time invariance — the same combination the notes report for $y[n]=x[-n]$.<br>Numerical verification: with the Gaussian test input $x_1(t)=e^{-t^{2}}$ and $t_0=1.5$, the response to the shifted input and the shifted response differ by far more than the numerical tolerance, while $\\max|y|\\le2\\max|x_1|$ holds throughout.',
  err:'Concluding that a stable system must also be causal, or that a non-causal system must be unstable; the four properties are logically independent.',
  teach:'Require one line of justification per property; a bare classification list cannot show whether the reflected term was analysed.' },

{ id:'Q2-10', module:'M2', kind:'exam', src:'pp. 11–14',
  stem:'A modulator implements $y(t)=x(t)\\cos(5t)$, with $t$ in seconds. Which single statement is completely correct?',
  opts:['Linear, memoryless, causal and BIBO stable, but neither time invariant nor invertible.',
        'Linear, memoryless, time invariant and invertible, with inverse $x(t)=y(t)/\\cos(5t)$.',
        'Non-linear, because the input is multiplied by another signal, but time invariant and BIBO stable.',
        'Linear, time invariant and BIBO stable, but not memoryless, because $\\cos(5t)$ depends explicitly on $t$.'],
  a:0,
  why:'Multiplication by a known function of $t$ is linear, memoryless, causal and bounded ($|y|\\le|x|$); but the explicit $t$ destroys time invariance, and $\\cos(5t)=0$ at $t=\\pi/10+k\\pi/5$, where the input value is irrecoverably lost.',
  wrong:{1:'The proposed inverse breaks down wherever $\\cos(5t)=0$, for instance at $t=\\pi/10$: there $y(\\pi/10)=0$ whatever $x(\\pi/10)$ was. Contrast the definition system $[\\cos(t)+2]x(t)$, whose gain satisfies $\\cos t+2\\ge1>0$ and never vanishes.',
         2:'Multiplication by a fixed, known function of time is a linear operation: $a x_1+b x_2$ maps to $a x_1\\cos(5t)+b x_2\\cos(5t)$. Non-linearity would require the input to multiply itself.',
         3:'An explicit $t$ in a coefficient makes a system time varying, not a system with memory. Only $x(t)$ is required to form $y(t)$, so the system is memoryless.'},
  hints:['Decide separately whether the rule uses input values at other times (memory) and whether it contains an explicit $t$ (time variance).',
         'For invertibility, look for instants at which the multiplying gain vanishes.'],
  sol:'Definitions.<br>Linearity: $S\\{ax_1+bx_2\\}=\\bigl(ax_1(t)+bx_2(t)\\bigr)\\cos(5t)=a\\,y_1(t)+b\\,y_2(t)$ — linear.<br>Memory and causality: only $x(t)$ is used, so the system is memoryless, and memorylessness implies causality.<br>Stability: $|y(t)|=|x(t)||\\cos5t|\\le B$ for every bounded input — BIBO stable, with bound $B$.<br>Time invariance: with $x_2(t)=x_1(t-t_0)$,$$y_2(t)=x_1(t-t_0)\\cos(5t),\\qquad y_1(t-t_0)=x_1(t-t_0)\\cos\\bigl(5(t-t_0)\\bigr),$$which differ unless $5t_0$ is a multiple of $2\\pi$. Not time invariant.<br>Invertibility: at $t=\\pi/10$ the gain is $\\cos(\\pi/2)=0$, so $y(\\pi/10)=0$ regardless of $x(\\pi/10)$. Two distinct inputs — the zero signal, and a signal that is non-zero only at $t=\\pi/10$ — produce the same output, so the map is not one-to-one. This is the continuous-time analogue of the definition argument for $y[n]=n\\,x[n]$, where $x[0]$ is lost.<br>Interpretation: amplitude modulation with a carrier that crosses zero periodically annihilates the input at those instants; practical receivers avoid this by adding a DC offset to the carrier, which is precisely what makes $[\\cos(t)+2]x(t)$ invertible.<br>Numerical verification: with $x_1(t)=e^{-t^{2}}$ and $t_0=1.5$, the response to the shifted input and the shifted response disagree well outside numerical tolerance, and $\\cos(5\\pi/10)=0$ to machine precision.',
  err:'Writing the inverse $x(t)=y(t)/\\cos(5t)$ without checking that the divisor never vanishes.',
  teach:'The decisive discriminator is whether the student checked the zeros of the carrier; everything else in this item is routine.' },

{ id:'Q2-11', module:'M2', kind:'graph', src:'pp. 11–12',
  stem:'A system is known to be linear. In a laboratory test the input is the single stem $x[n]=\\delta[n-2]$ (height 1 at $n=2$, zero elsewhere). The recorded output stem plot has height 1 at $n=1$, height 2 at $n=2$, height 1 at $n=3$, and is zero everywhere else. What does this measurement establish?',
  opts:['The system is not causal, and therefore it is not memoryless either.',
        'The system is not linear, because a single impulse cannot produce three output stems.',
        'The system is not BIBO stable, because the total output amplitude exceeds the input amplitude.',
        'The system has memory, but nothing about causality can be concluded from a single test.'],
  a:0,
  why:'For a linear system the zero input gives the zero output, so the input, which is zero for all $n\\le1$, could not produce the non-zero output sample at $n=1$ unless that sample depended on the future input at $n=2$.',
  wrong:{1:'A single impulse routinely produces a spread-out response — that is what an impulse response is. Nothing in the plot contradicts linearity.',
         2:'Stability concerns unbounded outputs from bounded inputs. A finite three-sample response of finite height is entirely compatible with stability, and gains larger than one are permitted.',
         3:'Causality is decidable here. Because the system is linear, an input that is identically zero up to $n=1$ must yield an output that is zero up to $n=1$ if the system were causal; the observed stem at $n=1$ rules that out.'},
  hints:['Ask what a linear system must produce when the input is identically zero.',
         'Compare the first instant at which the input is non-zero with the first instant at which the output is non-zero.'],
  sol:'Definitions (pp. 11–12): causal — the output at $n$ depends only on inputs at times $\\le n$; linear — superposition holds, which forces $S\\{0\\}=0$.<br>Step 1 — use linearity. Setting $a=b=0$ in the linearity condition gives $S\\{0\\}[n]=0$ for all $n$: the zero input produces the zero output.<br>Step 2 — restrict to $n\\le1$. The applied input agrees with the zero signal at every $n\\le1$ (both are zero there). If the system were causal, the outputs of two inputs that agree up to time $n$ must agree at time $n$. Hence a causal linear system would give $y[1]=0$.<br>Step 3 — compare with the measurement. The record shows $y[1]=1\\neq0$, so the output at $n=1$ depends on the input at $n=2$, a future sample. The system is not causal.<br>Step 4 — corollary. A memoryless system uses only $x[n]$ to form $y[n]$ and is therefore always causal. Since causality fails, memorylessness fails as well.<br>Interpretation: reading the stem plot, the response begins one sample before the excitation arrives — the signature of a non-causal system such as $y[n]=x[n]+x[n+1]$  or a smoothing filter with a symmetric window.<br>Consistency check: the recorded response $\\{1,2,1\\}$ centred on $n=2$ is exactly what the non-causal averager $y[n]=x[n+1]+2x[n]+x[n-1]$ would produce, which is a system of the type the measurement points to.',
  err:'Declaring the system unstable or non-linear because the output "is bigger" than the input; amplitude gain is unrelated to both properties.',
  teach:'Check that the student invoked linearity to fix the zero-input response — without it, no conclusion about causality can be drawn from one test.' },

{ id:'Q2-12', module:'M2', kind:'synthesis', src:'pp. 12–13',
  stem:'A temperature logger integrates its sensor reading with the accumulator $y[n]=y[n-1]+x[n]$, initially at rest. The sensor has a constant offset error of $0.02$ °C in every sample, so the input contains the bounded component $x_e[n]=0.02\\,u[n]$. Which statement describes the situation correctly?',
  opts:['The accumulator is causal and linear but not BIBO stable; the offset alone drives the output to $0.02\\times10^{4}=200$ °C after $10^{4}$ samples.',
        'The accumulator is BIBO stable, since every input sample is bounded by $0.02$ °C, so the output can never exceed $0.02$ °C.',
        'The accumulator is unstable because it is not causal: the running sum uses samples that have not yet arrived.',
        'The accumulator is non-linear, and the drift is a non-linear distortion product rather than a stability failure.'],
  a:0,
  why:'Back-substitution gives $y[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{n}x[k]$, which is causal and linear; the bounded input $0.02\\,u[n]$ produces $y[n]=0.02(n+1)\\to\\infty$, so BIBO stability fails and the drift after $10^{4}$ samples is $200$ °C.',
  wrong:{1:'A bound on each sample does not bound their sum. Summing $10^{4}$ terms of $0.02$ gives $200$, which is what makes the accumulator the standard counterexample to stability.',
         2:'The accumulator is causal: $y[n]=\\sum_{k\\le n}x[k]$ uses only present and past samples. Causality and stability are independent, and this system has the first without the second.',
         3:'The accumulator is linear — summation is a linear operation. The drift is the accumulation of a linear response to a bounded input, not a distortion product.'},
  hints:['Expand the recursion from rest: $y[0]=x[0]$, $y[1]=x[0]+x[1]$, and so on.',
         'Apply the BIBO definition to the specific bounded input $x_e[n]=0.02\\,u[n]$.'],
  sol:'Model (pp. 11–13): back-substitution of $y[n]=y[n-1]+x[n]$ from rest gives the accumulator$$y[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{n}x[k],$$which is linear (summation), causal (the upper limit is $n$) and has infinite memory.<br>Stability test with the offset alone: $x_e[n]=0.02\\,u[n]$ is bounded with $B=0.02$ °C, yet$$y[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{n}0.02=0.02\\,(n+1)\\xrightarrow[n\\to\\infty]{}\\infty.$$One bounded input with an unbounded response disproves BIBO stability, exactly as $x[n]=u[n]$ does  proof.<br>Engineering figure: at a sampling rate of 1 sample per second, $10^{4}$ samples is under three hours, and the accumulated bias is$$0.02\\ \\text{°C}\\times10^{4}=200\\ \\text{°C},$$which swamps any real temperature excursion.<br>Design consequence: a pure digital integrator can never be used on a signal with an uncalibrated offset. The standard remedies are to remove the offset before integrating, or to replace the accumulator by a leaky version $y[n]=\\rho\\,y[n-1]+x[n]$ with $|\\rho|<1$, whose response to a constant input settles at the finite value $0.02/(1-\\rho)$.<br>Numerical verification: cumulatively summing $10^{4}$ samples of value $0.02$ returns exactly $200.000$, and the running sum grows without bound as more samples are added.',
  err:'Assuming that a causal system built from bounded arithmetic must be stable; causality places no bound whatsoever on the accumulated output.',
  teach:'Look for the explicit bounded input in the disproof; a claim of instability with no counterexample input is not a proof.' },

/* ========= MODULE 3 — LINEAR TIME-INVARIANT SYSTEMS (pp. 14–21) ========= */

{ id:'Q3-01', module:'M3', kind:'concept', src:'pp. 14–15',
  stem:'An engineer measures the impulse response $h[n]$ of a black box and then predicts the response to every future input with $y[n]=x[n]*h[n]$. Under what condition is this prediction valid?',
  opts:['Only if the system is both linear and time invariant.',
        'For any linear system, since superposition alone already produces the sum $\\sum_k x[k]h[n-k]$.',
        'For any time-invariant system, since shifting the input shifts the response.',
        'For any causal system with finite memory, whether or not it is linear.'],
  a:0,
  why:'The derivation on p. 15 uses time invariance to turn the response to $\\delta[n-k]$ into $h[n-k]$, and linearity to superpose the weighted responses; removing either step invalidates the convolution sum.',
  wrong:{1:'Linearity alone gives $y[n]=\\sum_k x[k]h_k[n]$, where $h_k[n]$ is the response to $\\delta[n-k]$. Without time invariance there is no reason for $h_k[n]$ to equal $h[n-k]$, so the sum is not a convolution.',
         2:'Time invariance alone justifies $h_k[n]=h[n-k]$, but the weighted superposition of those responses requires linearity. A time-invariant squarer, for instance, is not described by any convolution.',
         3:'Neither causality nor finite memory is relevant. Causality only constrains the support of $h$ ($h[n]=0$ for $n<0$, p. 21); a non-linear system has no impulse-response description at all.'},
  hints:['Track the two properties used in the derivation from the representation property to the convolution sum.',
         'Ask which step converts the response to $\\delta[n-k]$ into a shifted copy of $h[n]$.'],
  sol:'Derivation. Start from the representation property$$x[n]=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}x[k]\\,\\delta[n-k],$$which is an identity for any sequence — no system property is used yet.<br>Step 1 (time invariance): $\\delta[n]\\to h[n]$ implies $\\delta[n-k]\\to h[n-k]$.<br>Step 2 (linearity, homogeneity): $x[k]$ is a constant with respect to $n$, so $x[k]\\delta[n-k]\\to x[k]h[n-k]$.<br>Step 3 (linearity, additivity): summing over $k$ gives the convolution sum$$y[n]=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}x[k]\\,h[n-k]=x[n]*h[n].$$Both properties are therefore indispensable, which is why the notes state that convolution can only be applied when the system is LTI.<br>Interpretation: $h[n]$ is a complete description of an LTI system precisely because every input can be written as a sum of shifted impulses. For any system outside the LTI class, one impulse experiment carries no predictive power.<br>Cross-check with the catalogue: $y[n]=(x[2n])^{2}$ is neither linear nor time invariant, and no impulse response can represent it; $y(t)=2\\pi x(t)$ is LTI, with the impulse response $h(t)=2\\pi\\delta(t)$.',
  err:'Assuming that measuring an impulse response is meaningful for any system, and applying convolution to non-linear or time-varying blocks.',
  teach:'Ask which step of the derivation each property supplies; naming both properties without locating them is a weaker answer.' },

{ id:'Q3-02', module:'M3', kind:'concept', src:'p. 21',
  stem:'Which statement about an LTI system and its impulse response is correct?',
  opts:['It is memoryless if and only if $h[n]=0$ for $n<0$.',
        'It is BIBO stable if and only if $h[n]$ is bounded for every $n$.',
        'It is BIBO stable if and only if $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}|h[k]|<\\infty$.',
        'It is invertible if and only if $h[n]\\neq0$ for every $n$.'],
  a:2,
  why:'Absolute summability is the stability criterion of p. 21: it gives $|y[n]|\\le B\\sum_k|h[k]|<\\infty$ for every bounded input, and it is also necessary (a standard result stated, but not proved, ).',
  wrong:{0:'That is the causality criterion. Memorylessness requires the much stronger $h[n]=a\\delta[n]$, so that $y[n]=a\\,x[n]$.',
         1:'Boundedness of $h$ is not enough: $h[n]=u[n]$ is bounded by 1, yet the accumulator it represents is unstable, since $\\sum_k|h[k]|$ diverges.',
         3:'The identity system has $h[n]=\\delta[n]$, which is zero for all $n\\neq0$, yet it is invertible. The correct criterion is the existence of $g[n]$ with $h[n]*g[n]=\\delta[n]$.'},
  hints:['Write down the bound $|y[n]|\\le\\sum_k|h[k]|\\,|x[n-k]|$ for a bounded input.',
         'Test each candidate criterion against $h[n]=u[n]$ and $h[n]=\\delta[n]$.'],
  sol:'Property list for LTI systems :$$\\text{memoryless}\\iff h[n]=a\\delta[n];\\quad \\text{causal}\\iff h[n]=0,\\ n<0;\\quad \\text{stable}\\iff\\sum_{k}|h[k]|<\\infty;\\quad\\text{invertible}\\iff\\exists g:\\ h*g=\\delta.$$Sufficiency proof of the stability criterion (given ): for $|x[n]|\\le B$,$$|y[n]|=\\left|\\sum_{k}h[k]x[n-k]\\right|\\le\\sum_{k}|h[k]|\\,|x[n-k]|\\le B\\sum_{k}|h[k]|<\\infty.$$Necessity is standard but is only stated in the notes, not proved — worth flagging when the "if and only if" is quoted.<br>Continuous-time counterpart: $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|h(t)|\\,dt<\\infty$ (absolute integrability).<br>Test cases: $h[n]=u[n]$ is bounded but not summable — unstable (this is the accumulator of p. 13); $h[n]=(0.8)^{n}u[n]$ has $\\sum_k|h[k]|=1/(1-0.8)=5<\\infty$ — stable; $h[n]=\\delta[n]$ has $\\sum|h|=1$ — stable, memoryless, causal and invertible at once.<br>Interpretation: absolute summability says the system has finite total "weight" to distribute over the input history; a system whose weights sum to infinity can accumulate a bounded input without limit.',
  err:'Substituting the easier test "$h[n]\\to0$" or "$h[n]$ bounded" for absolute summability.',
  teach:'The unit step as impulse response is the fastest counterexample to the bounded-$h$ criterion; expect it in a complete answer.' },

{ id:'Q3-03', module:'M3', kind:'concept', src:'p. 20',
  stem:'Two LTI systems with impulse responses $h_1[n]$ and $h_2[n]$ are connected (i) in cascade, and (ii) in parallel with their outputs added. What are the overall impulse responses?',
  opts:['Cascade: the pointwise product $h_1[n]h_2[n]$; parallel: $h_1[n]+h_2[n]$.',
        'Cascade: $h_1[n]*h_2[n]$, and the two blocks may be interchanged; parallel: $h_1[n]+h_2[n]$.',
        'Cascade: $h_1[n]*h_2[n]$, but the order matters because convolution is not commutative; parallel: $h_1[n]*h_2[n]$.',
        'Cascade: $h_1[n]+h_2[n]$; parallel: $h_1[n]*h_2[n]$.'],
  a:1,
  why:'Associativity gives $x*(h_1*h_2)=(x*h_1)*h_2$ for the cascade, commutativity ($x*h=h*x$) allows the blocks to be swapped, and distributivity gives $x*h_1+x*h_2=x*(h_1+h_2)$ for the parallel connection.',
  wrong:{0:'A pointwise product is not what a cascade performs; the second block convolves the first block\'s output, which is the convolution $h_1*h_2$.',
         2:'Convolution is commutative in both discrete and continuous time, so the order of two LTI blocks in a cascade is irrelevant. Adding two outputs is a sum, not a convolution.',
         3:'The two connections are interchanged. Summing outputs corresponds to adding impulse responses; feeding one output into the next corresponds to convolving them.'},
  hints:['Write the cascade output as $(x*h_1)*h_2$ and use associativity.',
         'Write the parallel output as $x*h_1+x*h_2$ and use distributivity.'],
  sol:'Properties of LTI systems.<br>Cascade: the intermediate signal is $w=x*h_1$ and the final output is $y=w*h_2=(x*h_1)*h_2$. Associativity gives$$y=x*(h_1*h_2)\\;\\Rightarrow\\;h_{\\text{eq}}=h_1*h_2,$$and commutativity, $h_1*h_2=h_2*h_1$, means the blocks may be placed in either order without changing the overall response.<br>Parallel: the two branches share the input and their outputs are summed, so$$y=x*h_1+x*h_2=x*(h_1+h_2)\\;\\Rightarrow\\;h_{\\text{eq}}=h_1+h_2$$by distributivity.<br>Worked instance: $h_1[n]=\\delta[n]-2\\delta[n-1]$ and $h_2[n]=\\delta[n]+3\\delta[n-2]$ give $h_1*h_2=\\delta[n]-2\\delta[n-1]+3\\delta[n-2]-6\\delta[n-3]$ in cascade, and $h_1+h_2=2\\delta[n]-2\\delta[n-1]+3\\delta[n-2]$ in parallel — clearly different systems.<br>Interpretation: cascade combines memory (supports add: $L_1+L_2-1$ samples from $L_1$ and $L_2$), whereas parallel only superposes (support is the union). The commutativity result is what allows a designer to reorder filter stages freely, provided every stage really is LTI.<br>Check: the special case $h_2=\\delta$ reduces the cascade to $h_1*\\delta=h_1$, as expected of an identity block.',
  err:'Multiplying impulse responses pointwise for a cascade — a rule that belongs to the frequency domain, which is outside the material covered here.',
  teach:'If a student cites a product rule, ask which domain it belongs to; that reveals whether the confusion is conceptual or premature.' },

{ id:'Q3-04', module:'M3', kind:'calc', src:'pp. 15–16',
  stem:'Compute $y[n]=x[n]*h[n]$ for $x[n]$ with values $2,-1,3$ at $n=0,1,2$ and $h[n]$ with values $1,4$ at $n=0,1$; both sequences are zero elsewhere.',
  opts:['$y[n]$ has values $2,\\,7,\\,-1,\\,12$ at $n=0,1,2,3$.',
        '$y[n]$ has values $2,\\,8,\\,-4,\\,12$ at $n=0,1,2,3$.',
        '$y[n]$ has values $8,\\,-2,\\,11,\\,3$ at $n=0,1,2,3$.',
        '$y[n]$ has values $2,\\,7,\\,-1,\\,12$ at $n=1,2,3,4$.'],
  a:0,
  why:'Superposing the scaled, shifted copies $2h[n]-h[n-1]+3h[n-2]$ gives $y[0]=2$, $y[1]=8-1=7$, $y[2]=-4+3=-1$ and $y[3]=12$.',
  wrong:{1:'These are the individual products with the overlaps never added: $y[1]$ omits $x[1]h[0]=-1$ and $y[2]$ omits $x[2]h[0]=3$. The defining feature of convolution is the summation of all contributions that land on the same $n$.',
         2:'This is the result of convolving with the reflected sequence $h[-n]$ (values $4,1$), i.e. the flip was applied to $h$ and never undone by the shift.',
         3:'The values are right but the support is not. The first non-zero output occurs at $n=0+0=0$, since both sequences start at $n=0$.'},
  hints:['Write $y[n]=x[0]h[n]+x[1]h[n-1]+x[2]h[n-2]$ and add the shifted copies stem by stem.',
         'The output support runs from the sum of the two starting indices to the sum of the two ending indices.'],
  sol:'Convolution sum : $y[n]=\\sum_{k}x[k]h[n-k]$. With a short $x$ it is easiest to superpose scaled, shifted copies of $h$, as  example (pp. 15–16):$$y[n]=2\\,h[n]-1\\,h[n-1]+3\\,h[n-2].$$Contributions, stem by stem:<br>$2h[n]$ places $2$ at $n=0$ and $8$ at $n=1$;<br>$-h[n-1]$ places $-1$ at $n=1$ and $-4$ at $n=2$;<br>$3h[n-2]$ places $3$ at $n=2$ and $12$ at $n=3$.<br>Summing the columns:$$y[0]=2,\\quad y[1]=8-1=7,\\quad y[2]=-4+3=-1,\\quad y[3]=12,$$so $y[n]=2\\delta[n]+7\\delta[n-1]-\\delta[n-2]+12\\delta[n-3]$.<br>Support check: $x$ occupies $n=0\\dots2$ (3 samples) and $h$ occupies $n=0\\dots1$ (2 samples), so $y$ occupies $n=0\\dots3$, i.e. $3+2-1=4$ samples — as found.<br>Independent verification: summing the samples gives $y$ total $=20$, which must equal $\\left(\\sum_n x[n]\\right)\\left(\\sum_n h[n]\\right)=(2-1+3)(1+4)=4\\times5=20$. A direct machine convolution returns $\\{2,7,-1,12\\}$.',
  err:'Listing the products without adding the ones that fall at the same output index, which produces the sequence $2,8,-4,12$.',
  teach:'The sum-of-samples identity is a fast marking check: the total of $y$ must equal the product of the totals of $x$ and $h$.' },

{ id:'Q3-05', module:'M3', kind:'calc', src:'pp. 15–16',
  stem:'The sequence $x[n]$ is non-zero only for $2\\le n\\le6$, and $h[n]$ is non-zero only for $-3\\le n\\le1$. Without computing any values, determine where $y[n]=x[n]*h[n]$ can be non-zero.',
  opts:['$-1\\le n\\le7$, so at most 9 non-zero samples.',
        '$-1\\le n\\le8$, so at most 10 non-zero samples.',
        '$2\\le n\\le7$, so at most 6 non-zero samples.',
        '$-3\\le n\\le6$, so at most 10 non-zero samples.'],
  a:0,
  why:'Every non-zero product $x[k]h[n-k]$ requires $2\\le k\\le6$ and $-3\\le n-k\\le1$, so $n=k+(n-k)$ ranges from $2+(-3)=-1$ to $6+1=7$, giving $7-(-1)+1=9$ possible samples.',
  wrong:{1:'Support lengths are not added directly: two sequences of lengths $L_1$ and $L_2$ convolve to a sequence of length $L_1+L_2-1=5+5-1=9$, not 10. The upper end is $6+1=7$.',
         2:'This keeps only the support of $x$. The negative indices of $h$ shift contributions to the left, so the output begins at $n=-1$.',
         3:'This is the union of the two supports rather than their sum. Convolution adds index pairs; it does not merge the two intervals.'},
  hints:['A term survives only if both factors are non-zero: $k$ in the support of $x$ and $n-k$ in the support of $h$.',
         'Add the smallest indices to get the start and the largest indices to get the end.'],
  sol:'Convolution sum : $y[n]=\\sum_{k}x[k]\\,h[n-k]$. A term contributes only when both factors are non-zero.<br>Step 1 — conditions on the indices:$$2\\le k\\le6\\quad\\text{and}\\quad-3\\le n-k\\le1.$$Step 2 — add the two chains. Writing $n=k+m$ with $m=n-k$, the smallest reachable $n$ is $2+(-3)=-1$ and the largest is $6+1=7$. For every $n$ outside $[-1,7]$ no pair $(k,m)$ exists, so $y[n]=0$ there.<br>Step 3 — count: $7-(-1)+1=9$ sample positions, consistent with the general rule$$L_y=L_x+L_h-1=5+5-1=9.$$Interpretation: convolution smears each sequence by the extent of the other. The output can start earlier than the input, because $h$ has support at negative indices — that is exactly the impulse-response signature of a non-causal system.<br>Note on "at most": the endpoints $y[-1]=x[2]h[-3]$ and $y[7]=x[6]h[1]$ are single products and are non-zero whenever those samples are; interior samples could still cancel, hence the phrase "at most".<br>Verification: convolving two indicator sequences with these supports on an index grid gives non-zero values exactly on $-1\\le n\\le7$.',
  err:'Adding the two lengths and reporting 10 samples, or ignoring the negative part of the support of $h$.',
  teach:'Ask for both endpoints, not just the length; the start index is where the non-causal part of $h$ shows up.' },

{ id:'Q3-06', module:'M3', kind:'calc', src:'pp. 18–19',
  stem:'Evaluate $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$ for $x(t)=e^{-3t}u(t)$ and $h(t)=u(t)$.',
  opts:['$y(t)=\\tfrac{1}{3}\\left(1-e^{-3t}\\right)u(t)$',
        '$y(t)=\\left(1-e^{-3t}\\right)u(t)$',
        '$y(t)=\\tfrac{1}{3}e^{-3t}u(t)$',
        '$y(t)=3\\left(1-e^{-3t}\\right)u(t)$'],
  a:0,
  why:'For $t\\ge0$ the overlap runs over $0\\le\\tau\\le t$, giving $y(t)=\\int_{0}^{t}e^{-3\\tau}d\\tau=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(1-e^{-3t}\\right)$; for $t<0$ there is no overlap and $y(t)=0$.',
  wrong:{1:'The factor $1/3$ from integrating $e^{-3\\tau}$ has been dropped. Check the initial slope: $y\\,\'(0)=x(0)h(0)=1$, which the accepted answer reproduces and this one does not.',
         2:'This is the integrand rather than the integral. Convolution with a unit step integrates the input; it cannot leave the exponential shape unchanged.',
         3:'Multiplying by 3 instead of dividing. As $t\\to\\infty$ the output must approach $\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-3\\tau}d\\tau=1/3$, not 3.'},
  hints:['Use $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ and note that $h(t-\\tau)=1$ exactly when $\\tau\\le t$.',
         'Two cases only: $t<0$ (no overlap) and $t\\ge0$ (overlap on $[0,t]$).'],
  sol:'Convolution integral : $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$.<br>Step 1 — flip and shift $h$. Since $h(t)=u(t)$, $h(t-\\tau)=u(t-\\tau)=1$ for $\\tau\\le t$ and $0$ otherwise. Combined with $x(\\tau)=0$ for $\\tau<0$, the integrand is non-zero only on $0\\le\\tau\\le t$.<br>Step 2 — Case I, $t<0$: the two supports do not overlap, so $y(t)=0$.<br>Step 3 — Case II, $t\\ge0$:$$y(t)=\\int_{0}^{t}e^{-3\\tau}\\,d\\tau=\\left[\\frac{e^{-3\\tau}}{-3}\\right]_{0}^{t}=\\frac{1-e^{-3t}}{3}.$$Combined result:$$y(t)=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(1-e^{-3t}\\right)u(t).$$Interpretation: convolving with a unit step is running integration, so $y$ is the accumulated area under $x$. It rises from $0$ with initial slope $x(0)=1$ and saturates at the total area $1/3$ — the step response of a first-order system with time constant $1/3$ s.<br>Checks: $y(0)=0$ (continuity, no impulse present); $y(\\infty)=1/3=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}x$; and $dy/dt=e^{-3t}u(t)=x(t)$, confirming that the operation inverted differentiation. A numerical convolution on a $10^{-4}$ s grid gives $y(1)=0.3167$ and $y(2)=0.3325$, matching $\\frac{1}{3}(1-e^{-3})$ and $\\frac{1}{3}(1-e^{-6})$.',
  err:'Forgetting the $1/3$ that comes from the antiderivative of $e^{-3\\tau}$, and thereby reporting a final value of 1 instead of $1/3$.',
  teach:'The final-value check $y(\\infty)=\\int x$ catches every scaling error in this item; encourage it as a routine last line.' },

{ id:'Q3-07', module:'M3', kind:'misconception', src:'pp. 15–16',
  stem:'For $x[n]$ with values $1,2,3$ and $h[n]$ with values $4,5,6$, both on $n=0,1,2$, a student computes $y[2]=x[0]h[0]+x[1]h[1]+x[2]h[2]=4+10+18=32$. What is the correct value of $y[2]$, and what went wrong?',
  opts:['$y[2]=32$; the calculation is correct, because at $n=2$ all three products line up.',
        '$y[2]=28$; the flip step was omitted — the correct alignment is $x[0]h[2]+x[1]h[1]+x[2]h[0]$.',
        '$y[2]=18$; only the product $x[2]h[2]$ contributes at $n=2$.',
        '$y[2]=90$; every product $x[k]h[m]$ must be summed at each $n$.'],
  a:1,
  why:'The convolution sum evaluates $h$ at $n-k$, not at $k$, so at $n=2$ the pairs are $(x[0],h[2])$, $(x[1],h[1])$ and $(x[2],h[0])$, giving $6+10+12=28$.',
  wrong:{0:'Summing $x[k]h[k]$ is a correlation-type inner product, not a convolution. It happens to reuse the same numbers, which is precisely why the error is so easy to make.',
         2:'Only one product would contribute if $h$ were an impulse. Here $h$ has three non-zero samples, so three products land on $n=2$.',
         3:'$90=(1+2+3)(4+5+6)$ is the sum of all output samples, not the value at one index. Each $n$ collects only the products whose indices satisfy $k+(n-k)=n$.'},
  hints:['Write $y[n]=\\sum_k x[k]h[n-k]$ and substitute $n=2$ before inserting any numbers.',
         'List the index pairs $(k,\\,2-k)$ for $k=0,1,2$.'],
  sol:'Convolution sum : $y[n]=\\sum_{k}x[k]\\,h[n-k]$. The steps are flip, shift, multiply and add.<br>Step 1 — set $n=2$: the second factor is $h[2-k]$, so the index pairs are $(k,2-k)=(0,2),(1,1),(2,0)$.<br>Step 2 — multiply and add:$$y[2]=x[0]h[2]+x[1]h[1]+x[2]h[0]=1(6)+2(5)+3(4)=6+10+12=28.$$Step 3 — the full output, for context:$$y[n]=\\{4,\\,13,\\,28,\\,27,\\,18\\}\\quad\\text{on }n=0,\\dots,4.$$Why the flip matters: $h[n-k]$ viewed as a function of $k$ is $h[k]$ reflected about $k=0$ and then shifted so that its origin sits at $k=n$. Dropping the reflection computes $\\sum_k x[k]h[k]$, which is the cross-correlation at lag zero — a different operation that coincides with convolution only when $h$ is symmetric.<br>Checks: the sample sum $4+13+28+27+18=90$ equals $(1+2+3)(4+5+6)=90$; the support is $0\\dots4$, i.e. $3+3-1=5$ samples; and $y[2]$ is the peak, as expected for the maximum-overlap position.<br>Note: had $h$ been symmetric, e.g. $\\{4,5,4\\}$, the flipped and unflipped calculations would agree — which is why symmetric test signals hide this error.',
  err:'Omitting the reflection and pairing $x[k]$ with $h[k]$, which computes a correlation instead of a convolution.',
  teach:'Ask the student to write the index pairs before the arithmetic; the flip error is visible there and nowhere else.' },

{ id:'Q3-08', module:'M3', kind:'misconception', src:'p. 21',
  stem:'A causal LTI system has impulse response $h[n]=\\dfrac{1}{n+1}u[n]$. A student argues: "$h[n]\\to0$ as $n\\to\\infty$ and the system is causal, so it must be BIBO stable." Which statement is correct?',
  opts:['The system is stable: a causal impulse response that decays to zero always yields a bounded output.',
        'The system is causal but not BIBO stable, because $\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\frac{1}{n+1}$ is the harmonic series, which diverges.',
        'The system is neither causal nor stable, since $h[n]$ is not absolutely summable.',
        'Stability cannot be decided from $h[n]$ alone; a bounded test input must be applied first.'],
  a:1,
  why:'The stability criterion is absolute summability, and $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n+1}$ diverges; concretely, the bounded input $x[n]=u[n]$ produces $y[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\frac{1}{k+1}\\to\\infty$.',
  wrong:{0:'Decay to zero is necessary but not sufficient. The harmonic sequence decays yet its terms accumulate without limit, so a bounded input can drive the output to infinity.',
         2:'Causality holds: $h[n]=0$ for $n<0$ by construction. Only stability fails, which shows the two properties are independent.',
         3:'The criterion is exactly a statement about $h$: absolute summability decides stability without any experiment. Applying one bounded input can disprove stability, but no single experiment is needed to apply the criterion.'},
  hints:['Apply the criterion literally: form $\\sum_{n}|h[n]|$ and ask whether it converges.',
         'Recall the behaviour of $1+\\tfrac12+\\tfrac13+\\dots$ — its partial sums grow like $\\ln N$.'],
  sol:'Criterion : an LTI system is BIBO stable if and only if $\\sum_{k}|h[k]|<\\infty$.<br>Step 1 — causality: $h[n]=0$ for $n<0$, so the system is causal. This settles one property and says nothing about the other.<br>Step 2 — absolute summability:$$\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}|h[n]|=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n+1}=1+\\frac12+\\frac13+\\cdots,$$the harmonic series, which diverges (its partial sums grow like $\\ln N$). The criterion therefore fails.<br>Step 3 — explicit counterexample. Take the bounded input $x[n]=u[n]$, $|x|\\le1$. Then$$y[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{n}h[k]\\,x[n-k]=\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\frac{1}{k+1}\\xrightarrow[n\\to\\infty]{}\\infty,$$a bounded input with an unbounded output — instability in the sense of p. 12.<br>Interpretation: the individual weights vanish, but there are infinitely many of them and their total is infinite. Stability is a statement about the total weight, not about the size of any single sample.<br>Numerical check: the partial sums are $2.93$ at $N=10$, $7.49$ at $N=10^{3}$ and $14.39$ at $N=10^{6}$ — growing steadily, consistent with logarithmic divergence and inconsistent with any finite bound.',
  err:'Replacing the summability test by the weaker condition $h[n]\\to0$, which every stable system satisfies but which does not imply stability.',
  teach:'Expect either the harmonic series argument or the $u[n]$ counterexample; a claim of divergence with neither is unsupported.' },

{ id:'Q3-09', module:'M3', kind:'exam', src:'pp. 18–20',
  stem:'An LTI system with $h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ is driven by the pulse $x(t)=1$ for $0<t<2$ and $0$ otherwise. Determine $y(t)$ on every interval and evaluate $y(3)$.',
  opts:['$y(t)=1-e^{-t}$ for $0\\le t\\le2$ and $y(t)=(e^{2}-1)e^{-t}$ for $t>2$; $y(3)\\approx0.318$.',
        '$y(t)=1-e^{-t}$ for all $t\\ge0$; $y(3)\\approx0.950$.',
        '$y(t)=1-e^{-t}$ for $0\\le t\\le2$ and $y(t)=2e^{-t}$ for $t>2$; $y(3)\\approx0.0996$.',
        '$y(t)=1-e^{-t}$ for $0\\le t\\le2$ and $y(t)=1-e^{-(t-2)}$ for $t>2$; $y(3)\\approx0.632$.'],
  a:0,
  why:'For $0\\le t\\le2$ the overlap is $[0,t]$, giving $\\int_{0}^{t}e^{-(t-\\tau)}d\\tau=1-e^{-t}$; once $t>2$ the pulse has fully entered, the overlap is fixed at $[0,2]$, and $\\int_{0}^{2}e^{-(t-\\tau)}d\\tau=(e^{2}-1)e^{-t}$, which equals $0.3181$ at $t=3$.',
  wrong:{1:'This never switches to the second case. After the pulse ends there is no further excitation, so the output must decay towards zero rather than approach 1.',
         2:'This treats the pulse as an impulse of area 2 applied at $t=0$. That approximation is valid only for $t$ far beyond the pulse duration; at $t=3$ it gives $0.0996$ instead of $0.318$, an error of a factor of three.',
         3:'This restarts the response at $t=2$ instead of continuing it. It also violates continuity: at $t=2$ it gives $0$, whereas the first branch gives $1-e^{-2}=0.865$.'},
  hints:['Flip and shift $h$: the integrand is $x(\\tau)e^{-(t-\\tau)}$ for $\\tau\\le t$, so the upper limit is $\\min(t,2)$.',
         'Three cases: $t<0$, $0\\le t\\le2$ (partial overlap), and $t>2$ (full overlap).'],
  sol:'Convolution integral : $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$, with $h(t-\\tau)=e^{-(t-\\tau)}$ for $\\tau\\le t$ and $0$ otherwise.<br>CASE I, $t<0$: the shifted impulse response and the pulse do not overlap, so $y(t)=0$.<br>CASE II, $0\\le t\\le2$ (the pulse is partly inside the window):$$y(t)=\\int_{0}^{t}e^{-(t-\\tau)}d\\tau=e^{-t}\\bigl[e^{\\tau}\\bigr]_{0}^{t}=e^{-t}\\left(e^{t}-1\\right)=1-e^{-t}.$$CASE III, $t>2$ (the whole pulse lies to the left of $t$):$$y(t)=\\int_{0}^{2}e^{-(t-\\tau)}d\\tau=e^{-t}\\bigl[e^{\\tau}\\bigr]_{0}^{2}=\\left(e^{2}-1\\right)e^{-t}.$$Evaluation at $t=3$:$$y(3)=\\left(e^{2}-1\\right)e^{-3}=\\frac{7.3891-1}{20.0855}=0.3181.$$Continuity check at the boundary: Case II gives $1-e^{-2}=0.8647$, Case III gives $(e^{2}-1)e^{-2}=1-e^{-2}=0.8647$ — the two branches agree, as they must for a bounded input and a bounded $h$.<br>Interpretation: the system charges towards 1 while the pulse is present, reaching $0.865$ at $t=2$, then discharges with the time constant of 1 s. The peak necessarily occurs at $t=2$, the instant the excitation is removed.<br>Numerical verification: a grid convolution with step $5\\times10^{-5}$ s returns $y(3)=0.31808$, matching the closed form to five decimals.',
  err:'Continuing the first-case formula beyond $t=2$, which lets the output keep rising after the input has been switched off.',
  teach:'Check the continuity test at $t=2$ first; almost every case-boundary error shows up there immediately.' },

{ id:'Q3-10', module:'M3', kind:'exam', src:'pp. 16–17',
  stem:'Compute $y[n]=x[n]*h[n]$ for $x[n]=(1/4)^{n}u[n]$ and $h[n]=u[n-2]$, and state $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}y[n]$.',
  opts:['$y[n]=\\tfrac{4}{3}\\left[1-(1/4)^{\\,n-1}\\right]$ for $n\\ge2$ and $0$ otherwise; the limit is $4/3$.',
        '$y[n]=\\tfrac{4}{3}\\left[1-(1/4)^{\\,n+1}\\right]$ for $n\\ge0$; the limit is $4/3$.',
        '$y[n]=\\tfrac{4}{3}\\left[1-(1/4)^{\\,n-2}\\right]$ for $n\\ge2$ and $0$ otherwise; the limit is $4/3$.',
        '$y[n]=(1/4)^{\\,n-2}u[n-2]$; the limit is $0$.'],
  a:0,
  why:'$h[n-k]=1$ requires $k\\le n-2$, so for $n\\ge2$, $y[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}(1/4)^{k}=\\frac{1-(1/4)^{n-1}}{1-1/4}=\\frac{4}{3}\\left[1-(1/4)^{n-1}\\right]$, which tends to $4/3$.',
  wrong:{1:'The delay in $h[n]=u[n-2]$ has been ignored. The output must be zero for $n<2$, since no product can be non-zero before the step turns on; this expression gives $y[0]=\\frac43(1-\\frac14)=1$ instead of $0$.',
         2:'An off-by-one in the upper summation limit ($n-3$ instead of $n-2$). It predicts $y[2]=0$, whereas the single surviving term at $n=2$ is $x[0]h[2]=1$.',
         3:'This treats $u[n-2]$ as $\\delta[n-2]$, so no summation is performed at all. Convolving with a step accumulates the input, so the output must increase towards a positive limit, not decay to zero.'},
  hints:['Determine for which $k$ both $x[k]$ and $h[n-k]$ are non-zero; the condition $n-k\\ge2$ caps the summation.',
         'Apply the finite geometric sum $\\sum_{k=m}^{N}ar^{k}=a\\frac{r^{m}-r^{N+1}}{1-r}$, valid for any $r\\neq1$.'],
  sol:'Convolution sum : $y[n]=\\sum_{k}x[k]h[n-k]$.<br>Step 1 — overlap conditions. $x[k]\\neq0$ requires $k\\ge0$; $h[n-k]\\neq0$ requires $n-k\\ge2$, i.e. $k\\le n-2$.<br>CASE I, $n<2$: the range $0\\le k\\le n-2$ is empty, so $y[n]=0$.<br>CASE II, $n\\ge2$:$$y[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}\\left(\\frac14\\right)^{k}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac14\\right)^{0}-\\left(\\frac14\\right)^{n-1}}{1-\\frac14}=\\frac{4}{3}\\left[1-\\left(\\frac14\\right)^{n-1}\\right].$$Step 2 — spot values: $y[2]=\\frac43\\left(1-\\frac14\\right)=1$, which matches the single product $x[0]h[2]=1$; $y[3]=\\frac43\\left(1-\\frac{1}{16}\\right)=1.25=1+\\frac14$, matching $x[0]h[3]+x[1]h[2]$.<br>Step 3 — limit: $(1/4)^{n-1}\\to0$, so$$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}y[n]=\\frac43=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\left(\\frac14\\right)^{k},$$the total area of the input, as expected when convolving with a unit step.<br>Note on the geometric formula: the definition states it with the side condition $|r|<1$, but the finite sum requires only $r\\neq1$; the restriction $|r|<1$ is needed only for the infinite sum used in the limit above.<br>Numerical verification: a direct convolution of the two sequences over $n=0\\dots39$ agrees with the closed form to machine precision, with $y[2]=1.0000$, $y[3]=1.2500$ and $y[39]=1.3333$.',
  err:'Summing to $k=n$ instead of $k=n-2$, which ignores the two-sample delay built into $h[n]=u[n-2]$.',
  teach:'The spot value $y[2]=1$ separates a correct derivation from all three off-by-one and no-delay variants.' },

{ id:'Q3-11', module:'M3', kind:'graph', src:'pp. 19–20',
  stem:'An LTI system is tested with the pulse $x(t)=1$ for $0<t<4$ and zero otherwise. The measured output is a trapezoid: zero until $t=1$, rising linearly to the value 2 at $t=3$, constant at 2 until $t=5$, falling linearly to zero at $t=7$, and zero thereafter. Assuming $h(t)$ is a rectangular pulse of constant height, determine $h(t)$.',
  opts:['$h(t)=1$ for $1<t<3$, zero otherwise.',
        '$h(t)=1$ for $0<t<2$, zero otherwise.',
        '$h(t)=2$ for $1<t<2$, zero otherwise.',
        '$h(t)=1$ for $1<t<5$, zero otherwise.'],
  a:0,
  why:'The output support $[1,7]$ has length 6 = 4 + (duration of $h$), so $h$ lasts 2 s and starts at $t=1$; the plateau height $2=A\\times2$ then fixes $A=1$.',
  wrong:{1:'A pulse starting at $t=0$ would make the output start at $t=0$, not at $t=1$. The output always begins at the sum of the two starting times.',
         2:'Height 2 over a 1-second window gives a total support of $4+1=5$ s, i.e. $[1,6]$, and a plateau of $2\\times1=2$ reached only after a 1-second ramp. The measured 2-second ramp rules it out.',
         3:'A 4-second $h$ convolved with a 4-second $x$ gives an 8-second output with a triangular (not trapezoidal) shape, since the two durations would be equal — inconsistent with the flat top.'},
  hints:['Read three numbers off the plot: where the output starts, where it ends, and how long the ramp lasts.',
         'For two rectangles the ramp duration equals the shorter width, and the total support equals the sum of the widths.'],
  sol:'Governing tool (pp. 19–20): the convolution of two rectangular pulses is a trapezoid whose ramp length is the shorter width and whose total width is the sum of the two widths.<br>Step 1 — start time. The output begins at $t=1$; since $x$ begins at $t=0$, $h$ must begin at $t_h=1$ s.<br>Step 2 — duration. The output support $[1,7]$ has length 6 s, and $6=T_x+T_h=4+T_h$, so $T_h=2$ s. Hence $h$ occupies $1<t<3$.<br>Step 3 — height. During full overlap the value is $A\\times\\min(T_x,T_h)=A\\times2$. The measured plateau is 2, so $A=1$.<br>Step 4 — consistency with the shape. The ramp should last $\\min(T_x,T_h)=2$ s: the plot rises from $t=1$ to $t=3$, and falls from $t=5$ to $t=7$ — both 2 s, and the flat top occupies $[3,5]$, of length $|T_x-T_h|=2$ s. Every feature agrees.<br>Result: $h(t)=1$ for $1<t<3$ and zero otherwise — a 2-second averaging window delayed by 1 s, which is causal ($h(t)=0$ for $t<0$, p. 21) and stable ($\\int|h|=2<\\infty$).<br>Numerical verification: convolving the two rectangles on a $2\\times10^{-4}$ s grid returns a trapezoid with support $[1.000,7.000]$, plateau $2.0000$ on $[3,5]$ and mid-ramp value $y(2)=1.000$ — matching the measured plot exactly.',
  err:'Reading the ramp duration as the width of $h$ but overlooking the 1-second offset, which yields a $h$ starting at $t=0$.',
  teach:'Require all three readings — start, total width, plateau — since any two alone are consistent with more than one rectangle.' },

{ id:'Q3-12', module:'M3', kind:'synthesis', src:'pp. 20–21',
  stem:'A room adds a single delayed echo, so the recorded signal is $y[n]=x[n]+0.5\\,x[n-3]$, i.e. $h[n]=\\delta[n]+0.5\\,\\delta[n-3]$. An echo canceller is an LTI system $g[n]$ satisfying $h[n]*g[n]=\\delta[n]$. Which $g[n]$ works, and is the canceller BIBO stable?',
  opts:['$g[n]=\\delta[n]-0.5\\,\\delta[n-3]$; stable, because it has only two non-zero samples.',
        '$g[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}(-0.5)^{k}\\,\\delta[n-3k]$; stable, because $\\sum_{n}|g[n]|=\\sum_{k}(0.5)^{k}=2<\\infty$.',
        '$g[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}(0.5)^{k}\\,\\delta[n-3k]$; stable, since the weights decay geometrically.',
        'No inverse exists, because $h[n]$ is zero at all but two indices and therefore cannot be inverted.'],
  a:1,
  why:'Convolving $h$ with the alternating geometric train telescopes: every echo term is cancelled by the next, leaving $\\delta[n]$; the weights are absolutely summable with total 2, so the canceller is BIBO stable.',
  wrong:{0:'This cancels only the first echo. Direct convolution gives $h*g=\\delta[n]-0.25\\,\\delta[n-6]$: a residual echo of $25\\,\\%$ amplitude reappears six samples late, so the identity $h*g=\\delta$ fails.',
         2:'The sign is wrong. With positive weights, $h*g=\\delta[n]+\\sum_{k\\ge1}2(0.5)^{k}\\delta[n-3k]$ — the copies add instead of cancelling, so this system doubles the echo train rather than removing it.',
         3:'An inverse does exist and is constructed below. Invertibility of an LTI system requires only some $g$ with $h*g=\\delta$ ; the sparsity of $h$ is irrelevant.'},
  hints:['Look for $g$ as an infinite train of impulses at multiples of the echo delay, and require the terms beyond $n=0$ to cancel.',
         'Once $g$ is found, test stability with the criterion $\\sum_{n}|g[n]|<\\infty$.'],
  sol:'Framework : an LTI system is invertible when there exists $g$ with $h*g=\\delta$, and it is BIBO stable when its impulse response is absolutely summable.<br>Step 1 — construct the inverse. Impose $y[n]=x[n]+0.5x[n-3]$ and solve for $x$ recursively:$$x[n]=y[n]-0.5\\,x[n-3]=y[n]-0.5\\,y[n-3]+0.25\\,y[n-6]-\\cdots,$$so$$g[n]=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}(-0.5)^{k}\\,\\delta[n-3k].$$Step 2 — verify the cascade. Using distributivity,$$h*g=\\sum_{k\\ge0}(-0.5)^{k}\\delta[n-3k]+0.5\\sum_{k\\ge0}(-0.5)^{k}\\delta[n-3k-3].$$Re-indexing the second sum with $m=k+1$ turns it into $-\\sum_{m\\ge1}(-0.5)^{m}\\delta[n-3m]$, which cancels every term of the first sum except $k=0$. Hence $h*g=\\delta[n]$.<br>Step 3 — stability of the canceller:$$\\sum_{n}|g[n]|=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}(0.5)^{k}=\\frac{1}{1-0.5}=2<\\infty,$$so the canceller is BIBO stable, with output bounded by $2B$ for any input bounded by $B$. It is also causal, since $g[n]=0$ for $n<0$ — so it can be implemented in real time as the three-tap recursion $x[n]=y[n]-0.5x[n-3]$.<br>Engineering interpretation: the echo strength $0.5$ is what makes the inverse converge. An echo of strength $\\rho$ gives $\\sum|g|=1/(1-|\\rho|)$, which diverges as $|\\rho|\\to1$ — a room with a near-perfect reflection cannot be equalised stably.<br>Numerical verification: truncating $g$ at 20 terms and convolving with $h$ returns $\\delta[n]$ to within $10^{-6}$ over the first 60 samples, while the two-tap alternative leaves the predicted $-0.25$ at $n=6$ and the positive-weight version diverges from $\\delta$ immediately.',
  err:'Stopping the inverse after one correction term, which merely postpones and attenuates the echo instead of eliminating it.',
  teach:'Require both parts of the answer — the telescoping verification and the summability check; an inverse that is unstable would be useless in practice.' }

]);
